1
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:06,180
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:07,410
We're going to go
ahead and get started.

3
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:08,370
I'm Emma Brunskill.

4
00:00:08,370 --> 00:00:13,100
I'm delighted to welcome you to
Reinforcement Learning, CS234.

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:15,940
This is a brief
overview of the class

6
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:18,060
and what we're going
to be covering today.

7
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:21,350
And I just want to start that
probably everyone's heard

8
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:23,190
of reinforcement
learning these days.

9
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:25,770
That wasn't true about
10, 15 years ago.

10
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:28,340
But you can describe what is
happening in reinforcement

11
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:31,400
learning by a pretty
simple statement, which

12
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,460
is the idea of an
automated agent learning

13
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:37,760
through experience to
make good decisions.

14
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:41,075
Now that's a pretty
simple statement to say.

15
00:00:41,075 --> 00:00:42,830
It sort of encapsulates
a lot of what

16
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:44,510
me and my lab and
many, many others

17
00:00:44,510 --> 00:00:47,780
have been trying to work on
for the last 10 to 15 years.

18
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:49,340
But it's sort of
deceptively simple

19
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,500
because it involves a
lot of different really

20
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:52,960
challenging and
important things.

21
00:00:55,570 --> 00:00:58,510
So the first is that any
sort of general agenda

22
00:00:58,510 --> 00:01:01,060
to try to achieve general
artificial intelligence

23
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:03,850
has to include the
ability to make decisions.

24
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:06,100
There's been absolutely
enormous progress

25
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,010
in what we would call
sort of perceptual machine

26
00:01:09,010 --> 00:01:12,820
learning, things like being
able to perceive faces or cats

27
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:14,930
or identify cars.

28
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:17,560
And we call that often
perceptual machine

29
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,420
learning because it
focuses on trying

30
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:21,440
to, say, identify something.

31
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,290
But, of course, in
reality, what we're all

32
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:24,970
trying to do all
the time is also

33
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:27,070
to make decisions
based on our perception

34
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:29,590
and based on our
information we're receiving.

35
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:32,470
And so it's critical if we
think about what it means

36
00:01:32,470 --> 00:01:36,100
to be intelligent to consider
how to make decisions, and not

37
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,420
just any decisions, but what it
means to have good decisions.

38
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:43,970
This sort of question over how
can we learn to make decisions,

39
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:47,860
particularly faced by
uncertainty and limited data,

40
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,630
has been a central question that
people have been thinking about

41
00:01:50,630 --> 00:01:54,340
at least since the 1950s,
particularly pioneered

42
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:56,450
by the ideas of Richard Bellman.

43
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,892
And we'll hear a lot more
about Bellman's equation, which

44
00:01:58,892 --> 00:02:00,350
many of you might
have seen before,

45
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:03,620
later even in this
lecture or next lecture.

46
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:05,690
So there's one sort of
argument for studying

47
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:08,030
reinforcement
learning, which is it's

48
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:09,690
an essential part
of intelligence.

49
00:02:09,690 --> 00:02:11,240
It has to be part
of a general agenda

50
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,560
of artificial intelligence.

51
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:15,740
And so we should study it
to try to understand what

52
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:17,610
it means to be intelligent.

53
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:20,532
And that certainly for me is one
of the really big motivations.

54
00:02:20,532 --> 00:02:23,240
So I think there's just a lot of
fundamental questions about what

55
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:26,420
is the data needed to learn
to make good decisions.

56
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:28,190
But there's another
really good motivation

57
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:30,920
to study reinforcement learning,
which is that it's practical

58
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,580
and it allows us to solve
problems we'd like to solve.

59
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:37,350
So in particular, over
the last roughly decade,

60
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:39,830
there started to be a lot of
really impressive successes

61
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:42,920
of using reinforcement
learning to tackle problems

62
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:45,770
or to get unprecedented
performance in a lot of really

63
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:48,020
important domains.

64
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:50,360
So the first one is
the board game Go.

65
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:53,360
So who here plays Go?

66
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:54,570
OK, a few people.

67
00:02:54,570 --> 00:02:55,110
Maybe not.

68
00:02:55,110 --> 00:02:57,193
You can talk to the people
that raise their hands.

69
00:02:57,193 --> 00:02:58,920
So it's an incredibly
popular board game.

70
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,170
It's also an incredibly
hard board game.

71
00:03:01,170 --> 00:03:03,380
It's far harder
than chess, and it

72
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:05,720
was considered a really
long outstanding question

73
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:07,520
of artificial intelligence.

74
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,860
But roughly like, I guess
about eight years ago now,

75
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,010
eight to nine years
ago, there was a team

76
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:15,590
at DeepMind, which was still
a fairly small organization

77
00:03:15,590 --> 00:03:17,720
at that point, that thought
that they could make

78
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:22,850
significant headway at teaching
AI agents to be able to play Go.

79
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:25,670
And the idea in this
case is that we're

80
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:28,730
going to combine between the
ideas of reinforcement learning

81
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:31,010
and Monte Carlo Tree
Search, which is something

82
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:33,560
we're going to hear about
later in this class,

83
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,260
to create a system
that played Go better

84
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,270
than any humans in the world.

85
00:03:38,270 --> 00:03:40,722
And so there's even a
movie now about sort

86
00:03:40,722 --> 00:03:44,660
of one of the seminal games
in that sort of endeavor

87
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:48,200
and how humans felt about that
and how the creators of the AI

88
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,790
systems felt about that.

89
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:52,400
But this feat was
achieved far earlier

90
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:53,850
than what people expected.

91
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:55,700
And one of the key
reasons for that

92
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:57,175
was using
reinforcement learning.

93
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:01,990
Another really interesting
place that we've

94
00:04:01,990 --> 00:04:04,510
seen progress of using
reinforcement learning

95
00:04:04,510 --> 00:04:06,160
to tackle incredible
challenges is

96
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:08,320
in the idea of fusion science.

97
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,720
Fusion is a potential
approach for trying

98
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:12,700
to tackle the huge
energy issues that we

99
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:14,320
have in trying to
transition to more

100
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:16,529
sustainable options for that.

101
00:04:16,529 --> 00:04:20,029
And one of the challenges here--
and I'm not a fusion expert--

102
00:04:20,029 --> 00:04:25,990
is to manipulate and sort of
control things within a vessel.

103
00:04:25,990 --> 00:04:28,750
And so what the reinforcement
learning question in this case

104
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:31,810
is, how do you command
the controllers, the coil

105
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:33,850
controllers, in order
to manipulate this

106
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:35,740
into different types of shapes?

107
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:37,842
And so this was a Nature
paper from two years ago

108
00:04:37,842 --> 00:04:40,300
where they showed you could
use deep reinforcement learning

109
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:42,610
techniques to accomplish
this in a way that

110
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:46,870
was far more flexible than
had previously been imagined.

111
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:49,210
One of my favorite examples
of the applications

112
00:04:49,210 --> 00:04:51,820
of reinforcement learning
comes from a pretty recent

113
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:55,240
important case, which
is COVID testing.

114
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:58,400
So this was a system that
was deployed in Greece.

115
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:00,100
They had limited
resources, and they

116
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,470
were trying to understand
who you should test in order

117
00:05:02,470 --> 00:05:05,710
to help control the epidemic,
because as many of you may know,

118
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:08,150
there's a lot of sort of
free movement within Europe

119
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:09,980
and there was a
lot of transitions.

120
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:11,605
And they were trying
to think about how

121
00:05:11,605 --> 00:05:13,930
to leverage their resources
in a data-driven way

122
00:05:13,930 --> 00:05:16,630
because, of course, the
epidemic was changing.

123
00:05:16,630 --> 00:05:20,230
And so this is a beautiful paper
by a Stanford graduate, Hamsa

124
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:21,500
Bastani, and her colleague.

125
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:23,290
She's a professor
over at Penn now

126
00:05:23,290 --> 00:05:26,810
that used reinforcement learning
to really quickly do this.

127
00:05:26,810 --> 00:05:27,940
And it was deployed.

128
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:32,920
So Greece used this for
their testing at the border.

129
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:36,790
But perhaps the most famous
example recently is ChatGPT.

130
00:05:36,790 --> 00:05:40,345
So I think that as
many of you might know,

131
00:05:40,345 --> 00:05:43,150
natural language processing
has had incredible successes

132
00:05:43,150 --> 00:05:44,830
over the last decade.

133
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:46,970
And there was a
lot of work trying

134
00:05:46,970 --> 00:05:50,300
to use transformers to
make really, really capable

135
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,160
natural language systems.

136
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:56,180
But up till around, I guess,
like a year and a half ago,

137
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:59,050
most of that work was not
known to the broader public.

138
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:01,370
So even though we were
getting these amazing advances

139
00:06:01,370 --> 00:06:03,650
in natural language processing,
it wasn't at the state

140
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:06,110
yet where everybody
was using it.

141
00:06:06,110 --> 00:06:09,080
And so the key
idea of ChatGPT was

142
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:11,030
to use reinforcement
learning to create

143
00:06:11,030 --> 00:06:13,910
vastly more capable systems.

144
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:16,970
And I like to talk about
ChatGPT not because it's perhaps

145
00:06:16,970 --> 00:06:19,680
the most well-known success
for reinforcement learning,

146
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:21,320
but also because
it exhibits a lot

147
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,600
of the different technical
challenges and questions

148
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,550
that we're going to be
covering in this class.

149
00:06:25,550 --> 00:06:28,010
So let's just walk
through sort of how

150
00:06:28,010 --> 00:06:29,870
at a very high level
sort of this figure

151
00:06:29,870 --> 00:06:33,050
from ChatGPT of how the
ChatGPT system works

152
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:34,640
in terms of training.

153
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,680
So the first thing
it does is it does

154
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:41,800
what we would probably
call behavior cloning

155
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:43,330
or imitation learning.

156
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:46,940
We'll be covering
that in this class.

157
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:49,560
And we'll be talking more
about it even in this lecture.

158
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:50,840
So what did it do?

159
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:53,600
So, again, just to remind, I
suspect everybody in this class

160
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:56,640
probably uses ChatGPT
probably multiple times a day

161
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:00,080
or Claude or Gemini or one of
the other large language model

162
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:00,810
systems.

163
00:07:00,810 --> 00:07:04,087
But just in case you have
not, the idea in this case

164
00:07:04,087 --> 00:07:06,170
is that you might have
some sort of prompt or task

165
00:07:06,170 --> 00:07:08,040
you want your
language system to do,

166
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:10,970
like explain reinforcement
learning to a six-year-old,

167
00:07:10,970 --> 00:07:13,580
and then someone
gives a response,

168
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:17,335
like we give treats and
punishments to teach, et cetera.

169
00:07:17,335 --> 00:07:19,460
And you can think you can
try this out with ChatGPT

170
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:21,860
and see how well you
think it explains it.

171
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:23,420
And then what that
was treated as

172
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:25,770
is sort of a direct
supervised learning problem.

173
00:07:25,770 --> 00:07:27,650
So just trying to
take that input

174
00:07:27,650 --> 00:07:30,030
and then to produce that output.

175
00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:33,050
And we will call that also
imitation learning or behavior

176
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:34,350
cloning in this class.

177
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:36,080
And we'll talk about why.

178
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,242
So that was the first step.

179
00:07:37,242 --> 00:07:39,200
And this is sort of what
people have been doing

180
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:41,040
in natural language processing.

181
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:43,460
And the systems were good,
but they weren't that good.

182
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:46,280
So the next idea was
to try to explicitly

183
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:48,960
think about utility
or rewards, like how

184
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:51,750
good were these
particular labels

185
00:07:51,750 --> 00:07:53,460
or these particular outputs.

186
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:55,600
So here we're going to
actually build a model.

187
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:59,940
We're going to build a
model of a reward which

188
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:04,350
relates to model-based
reinforcement learning.

189
00:08:04,350 --> 00:08:06,000
And the way we're
going to do this is--

190
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,292
or the way they did this is
we collect preference data.

191
00:08:08,292 --> 00:08:11,550
We ask people to compare or
rank across different forms

192
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:13,030
of outputs.

193
00:08:13,030 --> 00:08:15,240
And then we use that to
learn a preference model.

194
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:17,140
And we're going to cover
that in this class.

195
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:18,870
That's going to be one of
the differences to this class

196
00:08:18,870 --> 00:08:20,340
compared to a
couple of years ago

197
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:23,700
that I think preference-based
reward signals are really

198
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:25,240
important and very powerful.

199
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:27,407
And so we're going to be
covering that in this class

200
00:08:27,407 --> 00:08:28,020
this term.

201
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:30,027
So in this case, they
would learn a reward.

202
00:08:30,027 --> 00:08:32,610
And again, don't worry if you
haven't-- if you're not familiar

203
00:08:32,610 --> 00:08:33,730
with what rewards are and stuff.

204
00:08:33,730 --> 00:08:34,570
We'll go through all of that.

205
00:08:34,570 --> 00:08:36,070
I just want to give
you a high level

206
00:08:36,070 --> 00:08:37,836
sort of sense of how
ChatGPT is related

207
00:08:37,836 --> 00:08:40,169
to some of the things we're
going to cover in the class.

208
00:08:40,169 --> 00:08:41,919
So they learned a reward signal.

209
00:08:41,919 --> 00:08:44,790
And then they did
reinforcement learning using

210
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,680
that learned reward signal.

211
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:48,785
So now they're going to
do reinforcement learning.

212
00:08:52,010 --> 00:08:57,050
And this is called RLHF
because it is reinforcement

213
00:08:57,050 --> 00:08:59,090
learning from human feedback.

214
00:08:59,090 --> 00:09:01,730
And I'll just note here that
this was not the first time

215
00:09:01,730 --> 00:09:04,350
this idea was introduced.

216
00:09:04,350 --> 00:09:07,370
It had been introduced maybe
about four to five years

217
00:09:07,370 --> 00:09:11,780
before this for sort of
simulated robotics tasks.

218
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:14,630
But ChatGPT demonstrated
that this really

219
00:09:14,630 --> 00:09:17,630
made a huge difference
in performance.

220
00:09:17,630 --> 00:09:19,800
And so I think it's
a really nice example

221
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:22,050
of the types of ideas that
we're going to be covering,

222
00:09:22,050 --> 00:09:23,630
as well as sort of the
incredible successes

223
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:24,380
that are possible.

224
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:30,135
Now, even before
ChatGPT came along,

225
00:09:30,135 --> 00:09:32,510
there was starting to be a
huge interest in reinforcement

226
00:09:32,510 --> 00:09:33,830
learning.

227
00:09:33,830 --> 00:09:36,870
So some of you-- we have an
optional textbook for the class.

228
00:09:36,870 --> 00:09:38,160
It's by Sutton and Barto.

229
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:41,330
Richard Sutton is
from Canada and is

230
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,490
one of the sort of
founders of the field.

231
00:09:43,490 --> 00:09:45,630
And when I started in
reinforcement learning

232
00:09:45,630 --> 00:09:47,760
and I would go give my
talks in conferences,

233
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,430
it used to be like me and
Rich and 30 other people.

234
00:09:50,430 --> 00:09:52,378
Nobody cared about
reinforcement learning.

235
00:09:52,378 --> 00:09:53,670
I mean, it's not for, you know.

236
00:09:53,670 --> 00:09:56,045
A few of us did because we
thought it was really amazing.

237
00:09:56,045 --> 00:09:58,990
But as you can see, sort
of through the 2000s,

238
00:09:58,990 --> 00:10:01,650
which is when I was getting
my training around here,

239
00:10:01,650 --> 00:10:03,600
there just weren't
that many papers,

240
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,910
and the community
wasn't nearly as large.

241
00:10:05,910 --> 00:10:08,410
But this nice paper
by Peter Henderson--

242
00:10:08,410 --> 00:10:12,540
the y-axis is papers--

243
00:10:12,540 --> 00:10:14,940
shows that there's been this
sort of enormous increase

244
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:16,200
in interest.

245
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:17,850
And I think a lot
of this was really

246
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:20,940
due to the fact that
kind of around here

247
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:25,380
there was some amazing successes
on the Atari video games,

248
00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:27,750
where people showed that
you could learn directly

249
00:10:27,750 --> 00:10:30,340
from pixel input
to make decisions.

250
00:10:30,340 --> 00:10:32,770
And then there started to
be the successes in AlphaGo,

251
00:10:32,770 --> 00:10:35,580
and then there became
more and more successes.

252
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:38,470
So it is an incredible time
for reinforcement learning.

253
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:40,710
This curve is
continued to go up.

254
00:10:40,710 --> 00:10:43,440
However, I think it's also
important to notice that there

255
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:46,770
is also a number of skeptics.

256
00:10:46,770 --> 00:10:51,510
So there was a pretty famous
talk by Yann LeCun in 2016

257
00:10:51,510 --> 00:10:55,050
at one of the major machine
learning conferences, NeurIPS.

258
00:10:55,050 --> 00:10:57,370
So Yann LeCun, for those
of you who don't know him,

259
00:10:57,370 --> 00:11:00,360
is one of the sort of seminal
figures in neural network

260
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:01,150
research.

261
00:11:01,150 --> 00:11:02,860
He has won the Turing Award.

262
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:05,830
He is an amazing,
amazing researcher.

263
00:11:05,830 --> 00:11:08,485
So he gave a keynote at NeurIPS.

264
00:11:08,485 --> 00:11:09,610
I believe it was a keynote.

265
00:11:09,610 --> 00:11:11,110
He certainly gave
a very famous talk

266
00:11:11,110 --> 00:11:13,170
there, where he was
talking about the role

267
00:11:13,170 --> 00:11:16,950
of different types of machine
learning questions and subareas

268
00:11:16,950 --> 00:11:19,920
in terms of making progress
on machine learning.

269
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:22,050
And he very famously
talked about machine

270
00:11:22,050 --> 00:11:23,350
learning as a cake.

271
00:11:23,350 --> 00:11:25,380
And so he said that
the main cake is really

272
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:26,590
unsupervised learning.

273
00:11:26,590 --> 00:11:29,190
And that's really going to be
the body, the most important

274
00:11:29,190 --> 00:11:30,720
aspect of machine learning.

275
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:33,730
Things like representation
learning from unlabeled data,

276
00:11:33,730 --> 00:11:35,320
that's really going
to be the core,

277
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:37,150
and that's where we're going
to have huge amounts of data

278
00:11:37,150 --> 00:11:38,910
and we're going to
make a lot of progress.

279
00:11:38,910 --> 00:11:41,020
And then supervised
learning was the icing.

280
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:42,395
So that's still
pretty important.

281
00:11:42,395 --> 00:11:44,520
It's like very important
part of the cake, at least

282
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:45,680
in my opinion.

283
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:46,990
But it doesn't have as much.

284
00:11:46,990 --> 00:11:49,020
We don't have as much
supervised learning,

285
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:51,100
and it's sort of [INAUDIBLE].

286
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:53,400
And then he argued that
reinforcement learning was just

287
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:55,830
the cherry.

288
00:11:55,830 --> 00:11:59,520
Now cherries are important,
but not nearly as much,

289
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:01,030
perhaps, as the
rest of the cake.

290
00:12:01,030 --> 00:12:02,910
And he went on and
talked about some places

291
00:12:02,910 --> 00:12:05,310
where he thought that RL
still might have a role.

292
00:12:05,310 --> 00:12:08,370
But it was considered
a really important talk

293
00:12:08,370 --> 00:12:11,010
because what he was
sort of demonstrating

294
00:12:11,010 --> 00:12:13,110
is that reinforcement
learning was having

295
00:12:13,110 --> 00:12:15,160
a part to play in
machine learning,

296
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:17,302
but maybe only a
very minor part.

297
00:12:17,302 --> 00:12:19,510
Now I think it'd be interesting
to talk to him today.

298
00:12:19,510 --> 00:12:20,440
I haven't talked
to him recently,

299
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:22,590
so I don't know what
his current opinion is,

300
00:12:22,590 --> 00:12:24,570
but I think it's a
really important thing

301
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:26,563
to think about
like, where are all

302
00:12:26,563 --> 00:12:28,230
of these different
techniques important,

303
00:12:28,230 --> 00:12:31,200
and where will we be able to
make the most progress in terms

304
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:32,210
of advancing AI?

305
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:37,110
And so with that, we're
going to try and do

306
00:12:37,110 --> 00:12:39,180
our first poll, which is
about why you guys want

307
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:40,580
to take this class.

308
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:41,837
So we'll look through these.

309
00:12:41,837 --> 00:12:43,920
You'll have to bear with
us a little bit with-- we

310
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:46,782
had a few technical difficulties
that we're working with CTL on,

311
00:12:46,782 --> 00:12:47,740
but it should work out.

312
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:49,890
So if you go to either
the first link in Ed

313
00:12:49,890 --> 00:12:52,350
or you go to this
HTTP, you can--

314
00:12:52,350 --> 00:12:55,270
if you have any issues like,
do you want to be registered?

315
00:12:55,270 --> 00:12:57,730
If it's hanging, just skip
the registration, refresh,

316
00:12:57,730 --> 00:13:01,140
that should all sort it out,
and then just add in your SUN ID

317
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:03,600
as your screen, and
just take a second

318
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:07,690
and write down a bit about why
you want to take this class.

319
00:13:07,690 --> 00:13:08,840
And it could be anything.

320
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:10,700
It could be that you're really
curious about something.

321
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:12,610
It could be because
you're doing an internship

322
00:13:12,610 --> 00:13:14,235
and they told you
had to take something

323
00:13:14,235 --> 00:13:15,960
about reinforcement learning.

324
00:13:15,960 --> 00:13:18,642
Any of the things are fine.

325
00:13:18,642 --> 00:13:19,725
Just take a minute or two.

326
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:17,270
Thanks for all
the great reasons.

327
00:16:17,270 --> 00:16:19,700
I will talk about some of
those when I talk about also

328
00:16:19,700 --> 00:16:22,327
what we're going to cover
today and try to address why.

329
00:16:22,327 --> 00:16:24,410
I think a lot of the things
people are bringing up

330
00:16:24,410 --> 00:16:27,350
are things that we're
going to be touching upon.

331
00:16:27,350 --> 00:16:30,927
So I think if we
want to think about--

332
00:16:30,927 --> 00:16:32,510
I think it's really
important to start

333
00:16:32,510 --> 00:16:35,102
thinking about what is
reinforcement learning about,

334
00:16:35,102 --> 00:16:36,810
because if we understand
what it's about,

335
00:16:36,810 --> 00:16:38,840
then we know what
types of questions

336
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:40,170
we're interested in this space.

337
00:16:40,170 --> 00:16:42,230
And we also understand
what sort of applications

338
00:16:42,230 --> 00:16:43,940
it might be helpful for.

339
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:45,780
So, of course, your
creativity is unlimited,

340
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:48,030
so you can see what you might
come up with other ideas

341
00:16:48,030 --> 00:16:50,856
that people may not have
thought of for applied RL.

342
00:16:50,856 --> 00:16:53,307
But the four things
that people typically

343
00:16:53,307 --> 00:16:55,640
think about when they think
about reinforcement learning

344
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:58,520
as a discipline and as the
sort of what reinforcement

345
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:01,140
learning involves
is optimization,

346
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:05,720
delayed consequences,
exploration, and generalization.

347
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:08,690
So the first is optimization.

348
00:17:08,690 --> 00:17:10,700
And the optimization
aspect is really

349
00:17:10,700 --> 00:17:13,250
just saying that we're
thinking about the best way

350
00:17:13,250 --> 00:17:16,790
to make decisions, which
means that we explicitly have

351
00:17:16,790 --> 00:17:18,436
to have some notion of utility.

352
00:17:22,088 --> 00:17:23,630
An example of this
would be something

353
00:17:23,630 --> 00:17:25,005
like finding the
minimum distance

354
00:17:25,005 --> 00:17:27,569
route between two cities
given a network of roads.

355
00:17:27,569 --> 00:17:30,267
This means you can directly
compare different solutions,

356
00:17:30,267 --> 00:17:32,850
because if one solution has a
smaller distance than the other,

357
00:17:32,850 --> 00:17:34,550
it is strictly preferred.

358
00:17:34,550 --> 00:17:37,177
So there are many, many
important optimization questions

359
00:17:37,177 --> 00:17:39,260
and reinforcement learning
because it is concerned

360
00:17:39,260 --> 00:17:40,910
with making good
decisions, cares

361
00:17:40,910 --> 00:17:43,460
about us being able
to rank or decide

362
00:17:43,460 --> 00:17:46,340
across those different ones.

363
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:49,550
The second one is delayed
consequences, the idea

364
00:17:49,550 --> 00:17:51,320
being that the decisions
that we make now

365
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:52,980
can affect things far later.

366
00:17:52,980 --> 00:17:57,570
So maybe saving for retirement
now has some immediate cost,

367
00:17:57,570 --> 00:17:59,788
but it leads to some
significant benefit later.

368
00:17:59,788 --> 00:18:01,580
Or maybe there's
something you can do early

369
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:04,460
in a video game that later
has a lot of benefit.

370
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:06,890
There are two reasons
why delayed consequences

371
00:18:06,890 --> 00:18:07,970
is challenging.

372
00:18:07,970 --> 00:18:10,998
One is for the
reason of planning.

373
00:18:10,998 --> 00:18:13,040
Many of you might have
actually-- raise your hand

374
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:16,140
if you've taken AI at Stanford.

375
00:18:16,140 --> 00:18:17,310
OK, so about half of you.

376
00:18:17,310 --> 00:18:19,130
So you probably
saw planning in AI.

377
00:18:19,130 --> 00:18:21,530
And planning is the
idea that even when

378
00:18:21,530 --> 00:18:23,203
we understand how
the world works,

379
00:18:23,203 --> 00:18:25,370
it might be really complicated
to try to decide what

380
00:18:25,370 --> 00:18:26,960
the optimal thing is to do.

381
00:18:26,960 --> 00:18:28,650
So you can think
of this like chess.

382
00:18:28,650 --> 00:18:30,025
All the rules are known.

383
00:18:30,025 --> 00:18:32,150
It's still really complicated
to think about what's

384
00:18:32,150 --> 00:18:33,300
the right thing to do.

385
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:36,050
So when the decisions you make
involve reasoning not just

386
00:18:36,050 --> 00:18:39,390
about the immediate outcomes but
the longer term ramifications,

387
00:18:39,390 --> 00:18:42,080
the sort of planning
problems are even harder.

388
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:43,850
But the other reason
this is really hard

389
00:18:43,850 --> 00:18:45,980
is when we're learning,
meaning that we don't know

390
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:48,800
how the world works and we're
trying to understand how

391
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:50,540
through direct experience.

392
00:18:50,540 --> 00:18:54,600
So when we're learning, temporal
credit assignment is hard,

393
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:57,395
meaning that if you take some
action now and later on you

394
00:18:57,395 --> 00:18:59,520
receive a good outcome
or a bad outcome,

395
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:02,030
how do you figure out
which of your outcomes

396
00:19:02,030 --> 00:19:05,540
caused that good or
bad later result?

397
00:19:05,540 --> 00:19:08,120
So this happens all the
time to us as humans.

398
00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:09,870
How do you know why
you got into Stanford?

399
00:19:09,870 --> 00:19:10,662
Well, I don't know.

400
00:19:10,662 --> 00:19:13,310
Was it because you colored you
won a coloring contest when

401
00:19:13,310 --> 00:19:15,967
you were 6, because you
scored well on the SAT,

402
00:19:15,967 --> 00:19:17,550
because you went to
a good high school

403
00:19:17,550 --> 00:19:18,925
or you wrote a
really good essay?

404
00:19:18,925 --> 00:19:20,920
It's really hard
to understand this.

405
00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:22,900
In some cases, it
may be impossible.

406
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:25,115
But when we're getting to
make repeated decisions,

407
00:19:25,115 --> 00:19:26,490
it's really
important that we can

408
00:19:26,490 --> 00:19:29,310
start to use the prior
experience to figure out

409
00:19:29,310 --> 00:19:32,100
which decisions were important
or led to good outcomes

410
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:33,930
so that we can repeat them.

411
00:19:33,930 --> 00:19:37,300
So that's one of the
reasons why this is hard.

412
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:40,150
Exploration is one of my
favorite things in terms

413
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:41,540
of reinforcement learning.

414
00:19:41,540 --> 00:19:44,710
And the idea of this is
that the agent can only

415
00:19:44,710 --> 00:19:47,740
learn about the world
through direct experience.

416
00:19:47,740 --> 00:19:50,800
So it's like trying to learn
to ride a bike by trying

417
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:53,470
and failing and trying
again and through

418
00:19:53,470 --> 00:19:55,870
that direct experiencing,
learning the right way

419
00:19:55,870 --> 00:19:58,360
to ride a bike.

420
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:02,680
And the key idea about
this is that information

421
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:06,970
is censored in that you only
get to learn about what you try.

422
00:20:06,970 --> 00:20:08,680
So, for example,
right now, you don't

423
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:11,280
know how much worse your life
would be if you were MIT.

424
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:12,530
I went to MIT for grad school.

425
00:20:12,530 --> 00:20:14,380
MIT is also a great place.

426
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:16,990
But you generally
can't ever understand

427
00:20:16,990 --> 00:20:20,560
what that counterfactual
life would have been like.

428
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:23,260
It's one of the
central challenges.

429
00:20:23,260 --> 00:20:25,660
It's also a huge challenge
in causal inference, which

430
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:28,455
is another big interest of mine
and something my lab works on.

431
00:20:28,455 --> 00:20:29,830
So there's this
general challenge

432
00:20:29,830 --> 00:20:32,650
that you only get to learn
about the actual things

433
00:20:32,650 --> 00:20:36,670
that you do as an agent, as a
human, as an agent, et cetera.

434
00:20:36,670 --> 00:20:38,180
And so the question
is, how do you

435
00:20:38,180 --> 00:20:41,540
use that experience to figure
out how to make good decisions?

436
00:20:41,540 --> 00:20:43,075
So as a concrete
example of this,

437
00:20:43,075 --> 00:20:44,450
you can imagine
you're a company,

438
00:20:44,450 --> 00:20:46,918
and you give some promotion
to all your customers.

439
00:20:46,918 --> 00:20:48,710
You can't know what it
would have been like

440
00:20:48,710 --> 00:20:51,320
if you didn't give the
promotion to those customers.

441
00:20:51,320 --> 00:20:54,060
And even if you can give it to
one customer and not another,

442
00:20:54,060 --> 00:20:55,450
they are not the same people.

443
00:20:55,450 --> 00:20:57,960
So I can't rewind
and say, Dilip,

444
00:20:57,960 --> 00:20:59,877
who is our head TA,
this time, I'm not going

445
00:20:59,877 --> 00:21:00,960
to give you the promotion.

446
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:03,290
Let's see how that world
would have worked out.

447
00:21:03,290 --> 00:21:05,240
That's one of the
central challenges.

448
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:06,843
So we'll talk a lot
about exploration

449
00:21:06,843 --> 00:21:09,260
later because it's one of the
key things that is different

450
00:21:09,260 --> 00:21:12,410
compared to many
prior approaches.

451
00:21:12,410 --> 00:21:16,070
And generalization has
to do with this question

452
00:21:16,070 --> 00:21:18,210
of really wanting
to solve really big,

453
00:21:18,210 --> 00:21:19,460
challenging problems.

454
00:21:19,460 --> 00:21:22,040
So we'll talk a lot about
what decision policies are.

455
00:21:22,040 --> 00:21:23,790
But in general, you
can just think of them

456
00:21:23,790 --> 00:21:26,390
as a mapping from
experience to a decision.

457
00:21:26,390 --> 00:21:28,020
And you might think
in those cases,

458
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:29,250
you could just preprogram it.

459
00:21:29,250 --> 00:21:31,320
So if your robot goes
down the hallway,

460
00:21:31,320 --> 00:21:34,160
if it hits the end of
the hallway, turn left.

461
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:36,553
But let's think
about a video game,

462
00:21:36,553 --> 00:21:37,970
which we can think
of as just sort

463
00:21:37,970 --> 00:21:39,950
of generally having
some input image.

464
00:21:39,950 --> 00:21:44,000
So let's imagine that it's
something like 300 by 400.

465
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,250
And let's say we have at
least 256 different colors.

466
00:21:47,250 --> 00:21:51,110
So now we have set
of images that we

467
00:21:51,110 --> 00:21:55,520
could see that is at least
256 to the 300 cross 400.

468
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:57,360
So those are at least
the space of images.

469
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:59,180
That's probably
an underestimate.

470
00:21:59,180 --> 00:22:01,220
And now we get to
think about what

471
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:04,740
we would do in each of
those different scenarios.

472
00:22:04,740 --> 00:22:07,050
So the combinatorics are
completely mind-blowing,

473
00:22:07,050 --> 00:22:09,067
and we can't write
these down in a table.

474
00:22:09,067 --> 00:22:10,650
So this is why we
would need something

475
00:22:10,650 --> 00:22:13,350
like a deep neural network or
something else in order for us

476
00:22:13,350 --> 00:22:16,920
to try to make decisions in
these realistic settings which

477
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:20,860
are extremely large in terms
of the type of scenarios,

478
00:22:20,860 --> 00:22:24,930
the number of scenarios we
want to make decisions on.

479
00:22:24,930 --> 00:22:27,220
So you've probably seen
all of these ideas,

480
00:22:27,220 --> 00:22:29,610
or at least most of
them in other classes

481
00:22:29,610 --> 00:22:32,350
for other types of AI
or machine learning.

482
00:22:32,350 --> 00:22:34,080
So I think it's useful
just to contrast

483
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:36,450
what is reinforcement
learning doing

484
00:22:36,450 --> 00:22:38,250
compared to these other ones.

485
00:22:38,250 --> 00:22:40,510
So the first is AI planning.

486
00:22:40,510 --> 00:22:42,420
So in AI planning,
generally, we're

487
00:22:42,420 --> 00:22:45,480
doing some form of optimization,
trying to minimize a distance

488
00:22:45,480 --> 00:22:46,630
or something like that.

489
00:22:46,630 --> 00:22:51,403
We are often trying to
handle delayed consequences.

490
00:22:51,403 --> 00:22:52,820
And those are the
two main things.

491
00:22:52,820 --> 00:22:54,940
So we might also have
to do generalization

492
00:22:54,940 --> 00:22:58,450
if the size of the
space is really large.

493
00:22:58,450 --> 00:22:59,290
OK.

494
00:22:59,290 --> 00:23:00,920
So that is how--

495
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:03,950
so RL, in general, will
involve all of these.

496
00:23:03,950 --> 00:23:06,500
So this is how
those would compare.

497
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:09,090
If we think about something
like supervised learning,

498
00:23:09,090 --> 00:23:11,430
supervised learning
does involve learning.

499
00:23:11,430 --> 00:23:16,160
So we learn from data, whether
something is a cat or not.

500
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:21,240
And we have to do
generalization.

501
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:22,765
So we have those two things.

502
00:23:22,765 --> 00:23:24,390
And again, this is
going to be compared

503
00:23:24,390 --> 00:23:26,495
to reinforcement learning,
which has all of those.

504
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:31,060
In contrast to
supervised learning where

505
00:23:31,060 --> 00:23:33,000
you get the correct labels,
in unsupervised learning,

506
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:34,132
we don't get any labels.

507
00:23:34,132 --> 00:23:35,840
But we're still learning
from experience,

508
00:23:35,840 --> 00:23:37,920
and we're still trying
to do generalization.

509
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:44,220
Now the next
thing-- and this has

510
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:46,500
become a really popular
thing-- is to think about

511
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:50,460
whether we can map reinforcement
learning to imitation learning.

512
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:53,550
We talked about this really
briefly about ChatGPT,

513
00:23:53,550 --> 00:23:56,850
and we'll talk about a
lot more in the course.

514
00:23:56,850 --> 00:24:01,080
So in imitation learning or
behavioral cloning or reducing

515
00:24:01,080 --> 00:24:03,070
reinforcement learning
to supervised learning,

516
00:24:03,070 --> 00:24:07,190
we generally assume that we get
access to expert trajectories.

517
00:24:07,190 --> 00:24:08,750
So this could be
like someone saying

518
00:24:08,750 --> 00:24:10,750
what they would do in
response to those prompts.

519
00:24:10,750 --> 00:24:12,770
It could be someone
driving a car,

520
00:24:12,770 --> 00:24:15,590
and then you want to
mimic their behavior

521
00:24:15,590 --> 00:24:17,530
or some other similar example.

522
00:24:17,530 --> 00:24:20,240
So these ideas is that we
get input demonstrations

523
00:24:20,240 --> 00:24:21,680
of good policies.

524
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:24,470
And that allows us to reduce
reinforcement learning back

525
00:24:24,470 --> 00:24:26,720
to supervised learning.

526
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:29,285
So we're sort of taking this,
and we're reducing it back

527
00:24:29,285 --> 00:24:29,785
to here.

528
00:24:32,400 --> 00:24:34,620
I think, in general,
the idea of reductions

529
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:36,300
is incredibly powerful.

530
00:24:36,300 --> 00:24:38,528
For those of you that have
taken CS theory classes,

531
00:24:38,528 --> 00:24:39,820
that's what we do all the time.

532
00:24:39,820 --> 00:24:42,475
We reduce things to set
or other things like that.

533
00:24:42,475 --> 00:24:44,350
And, in general, I think
in computer science,

534
00:24:44,350 --> 00:24:46,058
it's one of the
strengths of it that they

535
00:24:46,058 --> 00:24:48,133
think of how can we reduce
one problem to another

536
00:24:48,133 --> 00:24:49,800
and then inherit all
the progress that's

537
00:24:49,800 --> 00:24:51,750
been made on that problem.

538
00:24:51,750 --> 00:24:53,580
So in this way,
reinforcement learning

539
00:24:53,580 --> 00:24:56,100
is similar to other
aspects of computer science

540
00:24:56,100 --> 00:25:00,270
in that we will try often to
reduce reinforcement learning

541
00:25:00,270 --> 00:25:01,510
to other problems.

542
00:25:01,510 --> 00:25:03,750
This is particularly done
in the theoretical aspects

543
00:25:03,750 --> 00:25:05,980
of reinforcement learning.

544
00:25:05,980 --> 00:25:06,850
Yeah.

545
00:25:06,850 --> 00:25:07,490
So just before--

546
00:25:07,490 --> 00:25:08,960
Whenever you ask--
just because I'm

547
00:25:08,960 --> 00:25:10,820
going to try and learn names,
could you say your name, please?

548
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:12,680
Yeah, my name's [AUDIO OUT].

549
00:25:12,680 --> 00:25:15,300
So just to be clear,
imitation learning then

550
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:17,230
isn't like a separate technique.

551
00:25:17,230 --> 00:25:18,930
It's just an application
of supervised

552
00:25:18,930 --> 00:25:22,385
learning to the specific
reinforcement learning context?

553
00:25:22,385 --> 00:25:23,260
It's a good question.

554
00:25:23,260 --> 00:25:24,300
So I think some of you--

555
00:25:24,300 --> 00:25:25,350
I mean, there's a
lot of techniques

556
00:25:25,350 --> 00:25:26,767
that think about
when you're doing

557
00:25:26,767 --> 00:25:30,010
imitation learning specifically
for kind of decision data.

558
00:25:30,010 --> 00:25:33,150
You can just think of it
just reducing it back.

559
00:25:33,150 --> 00:25:37,003
If you want to do imitation
learning where you might recover

560
00:25:37,003 --> 00:25:39,420
like the reward function--
we'll talk more about that soon

561
00:25:39,420 --> 00:25:40,180
and others--

562
00:25:40,180 --> 00:25:43,290
then you may need to use other
types of techniques as well.

563
00:25:43,290 --> 00:25:46,470
But the most straightforward
aspect of it is just to say,

564
00:25:46,470 --> 00:25:47,740
I've got demonstrations.

565
00:25:47,740 --> 00:25:52,980
I'm going to ignore sort of like
this delayed consequences aspect

566
00:25:52,980 --> 00:25:56,650
and exploration, and I'm
just going to reduce it back.

567
00:25:56,650 --> 00:25:57,150
Yeah.

568
00:25:57,150 --> 00:25:58,826
And name first, please.

569
00:25:58,826 --> 00:26:02,370
Wait, what do you mean by input
demonstrations of good policy?

570
00:26:02,370 --> 00:26:03,470
What does that mean?

571
00:26:03,470 --> 00:26:04,110
Great question.

572
00:26:04,110 --> 00:26:05,070
So let me give you an example.

573
00:26:05,070 --> 00:26:06,737
So people have thought
a lot about this.

574
00:26:06,737 --> 00:26:08,420
Maybe one of the first
examples of this,

575
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:10,545
or one of the first really
public examples of this,

576
00:26:10,545 --> 00:26:11,220
was for driving.

577
00:26:11,220 --> 00:26:14,455
Like at least what you could
do is I could drive a car.

578
00:26:14,455 --> 00:26:15,830
It could record
everything that I

579
00:26:15,830 --> 00:26:17,880
do in terms of controlling
the steering wheel.

580
00:26:17,880 --> 00:26:20,510
And then we could-- if I'm a
good driver, they could say,

581
00:26:20,510 --> 00:26:21,990
that's a good demonstration.

582
00:26:21,990 --> 00:26:24,980
So instead of the car trying
to learn from itself how

583
00:26:24,980 --> 00:26:28,290
to steer the wheel in order
to, say, successfully drive,

584
00:26:28,290 --> 00:26:30,080
you could have humans
drive it, and it

585
00:26:30,080 --> 00:26:33,200
could try to figure
out at each point

586
00:26:33,200 --> 00:26:37,640
how should I steer the wheel
in order to have good behavior.

587
00:26:37,640 --> 00:26:40,670
So the idea is that you
actually have access already

588
00:26:40,670 --> 00:26:44,850
to good demonstrations
of what is a good policy.

589
00:26:44,850 --> 00:26:45,350
Yeah.

590
00:26:45,350 --> 00:26:46,230
Name first, please.

591
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:52,200
What do you exactly mean by
optimization here [INAUDIBLE]?

592
00:26:54,570 --> 00:26:56,210
Optimization and what?

593
00:26:56,210 --> 00:26:58,820
What do you mean by
optimization and imitation?

594
00:26:58,820 --> 00:26:59,397
Ah, OK.

595
00:26:59,397 --> 00:26:59,980
Good question.

596
00:26:59,980 --> 00:27:02,190
So what I mean is that when
we do imitation learning

597
00:27:02,190 --> 00:27:05,250
from good trajectories,
we are assuming

598
00:27:05,250 --> 00:27:06,730
that we want to do well.

599
00:27:06,730 --> 00:27:09,310
So we want to actually
get a good policy.

600
00:27:09,310 --> 00:27:10,980
So imitation learning,
normally, we're

601
00:27:10,980 --> 00:27:13,770
not normally trying to
imitate bad performance,

602
00:27:13,770 --> 00:27:17,200
but you could think of this as
sort of reinforcement learning

603
00:27:17,200 --> 00:27:19,270
but without the
exploration part,

604
00:27:19,270 --> 00:27:21,250
because it's not trying
to pick its own data.

605
00:27:21,250 --> 00:27:23,280
Why don't supervised
learning and

606
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:26,020
unsupervised have that thing?

607
00:27:26,020 --> 00:27:27,380
Have the optimization?

608
00:27:27,380 --> 00:27:28,720
Yeah.

609
00:27:28,720 --> 00:27:30,550
So I think because we
normally don't have

610
00:27:30,550 --> 00:27:33,460
the notion of utility in those.

611
00:27:33,460 --> 00:27:35,810
So you might say this is
a cat or it's not a cat.

612
00:27:35,810 --> 00:27:38,180
It's not like a good
picture of a cat or not.

613
00:27:38,180 --> 00:27:41,380
Whereas in decisions, we often
have a real valued scalar value

614
00:27:41,380 --> 00:27:46,008
of like it was like
a 0.7 good decision.

615
00:27:46,008 --> 00:27:47,050
Yeah, name first, please.

616
00:27:47,050 --> 00:27:51,750
[INAUDIBLE] also have
the loss function,

617
00:27:51,750 --> 00:27:53,750
which we intend to optimize.

618
00:27:53,750 --> 00:27:54,410
Yes.

619
00:27:54,410 --> 00:27:57,090
So we do often-- we always
have loss functions.

620
00:27:57,090 --> 00:27:59,370
And that's a great--
but in those cases,

621
00:27:59,370 --> 00:28:01,190
there's not normally
a utility that goes.

622
00:28:01,190 --> 00:28:03,200
So if you could get--

623
00:28:03,200 --> 00:28:05,360
you could maybe have
some smooth notion there

624
00:28:05,360 --> 00:28:07,920
of how well do you match
like a stochastic policy,

625
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:09,980
a stochastic output there.

626
00:28:09,980 --> 00:28:12,680
But for many of those,
it would be more--

627
00:28:12,680 --> 00:28:14,690
if it's like, did you
say it was a cat or not,

628
00:28:14,690 --> 00:28:18,120
you would have a
binary 0-1 loss.

629
00:28:18,120 --> 00:28:18,810
Yeah.

630
00:28:18,810 --> 00:28:19,310
Hi.

631
00:28:19,310 --> 00:28:20,410
[AUDIO OUT]

632
00:28:20,410 --> 00:28:23,410
So does that mean, if you have
the data for imitation learning,

633
00:28:23,410 --> 00:28:26,620
it's like almost always better
than reinforcement learning?

634
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:29,590
Because avoiding the
fasteners, you can directly

635
00:28:29,590 --> 00:28:32,440
learn what is good.

636
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:33,610
Say that again.

637
00:28:33,610 --> 00:28:36,350
So if you have the data
for imitation learning,

638
00:28:36,350 --> 00:28:38,560
if you have someone
actually driving the car,

639
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:42,250
does that mean that you
will probably learn a better

640
00:28:42,250 --> 00:28:44,170
policy than
reinforcement learning

641
00:28:44,170 --> 00:28:45,710
or that it's almost
always better?

642
00:28:45,710 --> 00:28:46,370
Great question.

643
00:28:46,370 --> 00:28:47,060
So we'll get into that.

644
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:48,070
So the question
was-- if you can hear

645
00:28:48,070 --> 00:28:49,778
that-- is if you have
good demonstration,

646
00:28:49,778 --> 00:28:52,370
say, of driving behavior that
you're using imitation learning,

647
00:28:52,370 --> 00:28:54,350
can that be better than
reinforcement learning?

648
00:28:54,350 --> 00:28:55,630
It will depend on your
reinforcement learning

649
00:28:55,630 --> 00:28:56,193
algorithm.

650
00:28:56,193 --> 00:28:57,610
In general,
reinforcement learning

651
00:28:57,610 --> 00:29:01,870
should always be able
to equal or exceed

652
00:29:01,870 --> 00:29:03,910
the performance of
imitation learning.

653
00:29:03,910 --> 00:29:05,460
Yeah.

654
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:10,920
So can you explain the
difference between IL and RLHF?

655
00:29:10,920 --> 00:29:11,950
Yes, great question.

656
00:29:11,950 --> 00:29:15,090
So in imitation learning,
what we would have--

657
00:29:15,090 --> 00:29:16,980
and this is what-- this
was the first part.

658
00:29:16,980 --> 00:29:19,710
You would say people give me--

659
00:29:19,710 --> 00:29:21,570
given a prompt, I
look on the internet,

660
00:29:21,570 --> 00:29:23,190
and I assume that
those were good.

661
00:29:23,190 --> 00:29:25,230
So in the internet,
I see if someone

662
00:29:25,230 --> 00:29:27,347
said like how to
explain reinforcement

663
00:29:27,347 --> 00:29:29,680
learning to a six-year-old,
this is what they said back.

664
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:31,470
And so I just train on those.

665
00:29:31,470 --> 00:29:36,070
What RLHF said is that, well,
the internet is a big place.

666
00:29:36,070 --> 00:29:37,780
Probably not all of
it is good answers.

667
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:40,710
So now let's actually ask people
which of these two responses

668
00:29:40,710 --> 00:29:41,223
they prefer.

669
00:29:41,223 --> 00:29:43,140
And now we're going to
try to do reinforcement

670
00:29:43,140 --> 00:29:46,680
learning on that to actually
get to a better policy.

671
00:29:46,680 --> 00:29:48,876
Yeah.

672
00:29:48,876 --> 00:29:51,640
Something I'd like to
ask-- so AlphaGo actually

673
00:29:51,640 --> 00:29:55,810
discovers some Go strategies
that are not invented by humans

674
00:29:55,810 --> 00:29:57,840
that we have never
experienced before.

675
00:29:57,840 --> 00:30:01,370
So does it mean that if we apply
imitation learning too much,

676
00:30:01,370 --> 00:30:03,520
it might actually hinder
the model's capabilities

677
00:30:03,520 --> 00:30:05,890
to explore like what
is actually good

678
00:30:05,890 --> 00:30:07,790
instead of what humans
have thought of,

679
00:30:07,790 --> 00:30:09,310
which is probably wrong?

680
00:30:09,310 --> 00:30:09,820
Absolutely.

681
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:11,360
And actually, I think
this is on the next slide.

682
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:11,660
Let's go back.

683
00:30:11,660 --> 00:30:12,230
Good.

684
00:30:12,230 --> 00:30:13,070
OK, perfect.

685
00:30:13,070 --> 00:30:15,082
So this turns as to where
are some of the places

686
00:30:15,082 --> 00:30:17,290
that you might hope that
reinforcement learning would

687
00:30:17,290 --> 00:30:19,390
be better than these
other strategies.

688
00:30:19,390 --> 00:30:21,160
So one of them is
where you don't have

689
00:30:21,160 --> 00:30:22,582
examples of desired behavior.

690
00:30:22,582 --> 00:30:24,040
So this is exactly
like the example

691
00:30:24,040 --> 00:30:25,082
that was just brought up.

692
00:30:25,082 --> 00:30:27,140
If you want to go beyond
human performance,

693
00:30:27,140 --> 00:30:30,010
you cannot rely on human
performance just to do imitation

694
00:30:30,010 --> 00:30:32,890
learning because you're not
going to be able to get better

695
00:30:32,890 --> 00:30:33,610
than it.

696
00:30:33,610 --> 00:30:35,510
So there are a lot
of application areas,

697
00:30:35,510 --> 00:30:38,890
I think, particularly in areas
like health care or education

698
00:30:38,890 --> 00:30:42,460
and others where we think we
can go beyond human performance.

699
00:30:42,460 --> 00:30:44,140
And so in those
cases, reinforcement

700
00:30:44,140 --> 00:30:46,960
learning because it's trying
to optimize performance

701
00:30:46,960 --> 00:30:47,920
could go beyond.

702
00:30:47,920 --> 00:30:50,540
It could be a particularly
useful technique.

703
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:53,185
Another is where you don't have
any existing data for a task.

704
00:30:53,185 --> 00:30:54,560
So there might be
something where

705
00:30:54,560 --> 00:30:57,030
you think of it as a
decision-making problem,

706
00:30:57,030 --> 00:30:58,280
but you don't have prior data.

707
00:30:58,280 --> 00:31:00,080
And you need to
learn from scratch,

708
00:31:00,080 --> 00:31:01,723
and you want to
directly optimize.

709
00:31:01,723 --> 00:31:03,890
So that's another place
where reinforcement learning

710
00:31:03,890 --> 00:31:05,930
can be very powerful.

711
00:31:05,930 --> 00:31:09,360
Another category is interesting
because in some ways,

712
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:11,370
it's also kind of a
reduction technique.

713
00:31:11,370 --> 00:31:14,240
And this is the place where
we have an enormous search

714
00:31:14,240 --> 00:31:17,150
or optimization problem
with delayed outcomes.

715
00:31:17,150 --> 00:31:19,190
So there's been a
number of examples

716
00:31:19,190 --> 00:31:21,860
of the work of doing this
from DeepMind, which have

717
00:31:21,860 --> 00:31:23,730
been really extremely elegant.

718
00:31:23,730 --> 00:31:26,270
So what I put up
here is AlphaTensor.

719
00:31:26,270 --> 00:31:28,550
If you haven't heard of
it, it's a faster way

720
00:31:28,550 --> 00:31:31,830
to do matrix multiplication,
which is kind of mind-blowing.

721
00:31:31,830 --> 00:31:33,690
So what they did is
they said, all right,

722
00:31:33,690 --> 00:31:35,850
there's standard ways to
do matrix multiplication.

723
00:31:35,850 --> 00:31:37,170
This comes up all the time.

724
00:31:37,170 --> 00:31:40,250
Could we learn an algorithm
that would be better

725
00:31:40,250 --> 00:31:41,700
at matrix multiplication?

726
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:44,400
Not me as like a scientist try
to write down an algorithm.

727
00:31:44,400 --> 00:31:47,270
Have an agent learn one.

728
00:31:47,270 --> 00:31:48,800
And they showed yes.

729
00:31:48,800 --> 00:31:51,428
And the way they did that was
with reinforcement learning.

730
00:31:51,428 --> 00:31:52,970
And they've done
this in other cases,

731
00:31:52,970 --> 00:31:55,910
too, like learning faster,
sorting algorithms.

732
00:31:55,910 --> 00:31:58,145
So I think this is a
pretty incredible frontier.

733
00:31:58,145 --> 00:32:00,020
The idea is saying,
could we have AI actually

734
00:32:00,020 --> 00:32:02,510
be inventing new algorithms?

735
00:32:02,510 --> 00:32:05,340
And one of the ways that
they framed it here--

736
00:32:05,340 --> 00:32:07,290
and you can think of
AlphaGo as similar--

737
00:32:07,290 --> 00:32:11,140
is that it was a really, really,
really large search problem.

738
00:32:11,140 --> 00:32:13,690
And the challenge with really,
really large search problems

739
00:32:13,690 --> 00:32:16,390
is that even there, we may
not have great techniques

740
00:32:16,390 --> 00:32:17,690
for solving them.

741
00:32:17,690 --> 00:32:19,658
And so it's sort of a reduction.

742
00:32:19,658 --> 00:32:21,700
You can think of people
taking a planning problem

743
00:32:21,700 --> 00:32:24,490
and trying to reduce it to a
reinforcement learning problem

744
00:32:24,490 --> 00:32:26,320
to make it more tractable.

745
00:32:26,320 --> 00:32:27,395
So that's pretty wild.

746
00:32:27,395 --> 00:32:29,020
Most of the time we
think of sort of RL

747
00:32:29,020 --> 00:32:31,480
been reduced in the other
direction or involving planning

748
00:32:31,480 --> 00:32:32,270
or above that.

749
00:32:32,270 --> 00:32:33,880
But here, in some
ways, you can think

750
00:32:33,880 --> 00:32:36,670
of these as like either
adversarial planning

751
00:32:36,670 --> 00:32:39,490
problems or Expectimax problems
that are being reduced back

752
00:32:39,490 --> 00:32:42,220
to learning as a way to
just more efficiently go

753
00:32:42,220 --> 00:32:43,780
through the search space.

