model represent in this case.

1005
00:42:15,620 --> 00:42:17,320
And then in
particular, what would

1006
00:42:17,320 --> 00:42:20,560
a policy to optimize
the expected discounted

1007
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:23,440
sum of rewards do
in this case for how

1008
00:42:23,440 --> 00:42:25,780
I've set up this scenario?

1009
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:27,590
So I'd like you to
write down your answers,

1010
00:42:27,590 --> 00:42:29,020
enter them into
Ed, and then we're

1011
00:42:29,020 --> 00:42:32,750
going to do some small group
discussion in about 5 minutes.

1012
00:43:49,330 --> 00:43:51,580
And if you're not familiar
with these particular words

1013
00:43:51,580 --> 00:43:52,955
like state space,
et cetera, it's

1014
00:43:52,955 --> 00:43:55,030
still fine just to
think about, given

1015
00:43:55,030 --> 00:43:57,550
what I've told you about the
reward for an agent, what

1016
00:43:57,550 --> 00:43:58,710
might happen in this case?

1017
00:45:17,680 --> 00:45:21,480
So [INAUDIBLE] only
gives the first question.

1018
00:45:21,480 --> 00:45:22,100
Ah, OK.

1019
00:45:22,100 --> 00:45:22,600
Sorry.

1020
00:45:22,600 --> 00:45:24,187
You might have to
switch to the Ed.

1021
00:45:24,187 --> 00:45:24,687
OK.

1022
00:47:12,440 --> 00:47:12,940
All right.

1023
00:47:12,940 --> 00:47:13,990
Try to enter in something.

1024
00:47:13,990 --> 00:47:15,115
It's OK if you're not sure.

1025
00:47:15,115 --> 00:47:18,120
And then turn to someone near
you and compare what you did.

1026
00:47:22,826 --> 00:47:25,790
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

1027
00:50:14,210 --> 00:50:14,720
All right.

1028
00:50:14,720 --> 00:50:15,762
We're going to come back.

1029
00:50:15,762 --> 00:50:19,480
Hopefully, I heard a lot of
really fruitful discussions.

1030
00:50:19,480 --> 00:50:22,310
So let's see.

1031
00:50:22,310 --> 00:50:23,830
I know at least
one group I talked

1032
00:50:23,830 --> 00:50:27,393
to had a great idea for what
the state space could be.

1033
00:50:27,393 --> 00:50:29,560
Do you guys want to share
what your state space was?

1034
00:50:29,560 --> 00:50:31,500
And maybe tell
your name as well.

1035
00:50:31,500 --> 00:50:32,000
Sure.

1036
00:50:34,720 --> 00:50:38,140
You said the state space can be
just like a set of word pairs

1037
00:50:38,140 --> 00:50:40,810
of like two natural numbers
or any kind of numbers

1038
00:50:40,810 --> 00:50:43,960
of like how good the student
is at addition than how good

1039
00:50:43,960 --> 00:50:47,180
the student is at subtraction.

1040
00:50:47,180 --> 00:50:47,680
Yeah.

1041
00:50:47,680 --> 00:50:49,390
So you could imagine
something which

1042
00:50:49,390 --> 00:50:52,888
is at addition and subtraction.

1043
00:50:52,888 --> 00:50:55,180
So you could imagine something
like this where you just

1044
00:50:55,180 --> 00:50:58,030
have a vector pair where
it's like maybe they're

1045
00:50:58,030 --> 00:51:01,400
0.9 close to mastery for
addition and like 0.4

1046
00:51:01,400 --> 00:51:04,010
close to mastery
for subtraction.

1047
00:51:04,010 --> 00:51:06,050
This is not the only way
you could write down.

1048
00:51:06,050 --> 00:51:07,460
There's lots of choices
for the state space,

1049
00:51:07,460 --> 00:51:09,610
but that would certainly
be one reasonable one.

1050
00:51:09,610 --> 00:51:12,093
Those are challenging
in some ways

1051
00:51:12,093 --> 00:51:13,760
because you can't
directly observe them,

1052
00:51:13,760 --> 00:51:16,220
but it's a pretty natural
way to write it down.

1053
00:51:16,220 --> 00:51:18,010
And in fact, there
are commercial systems

1054
00:51:18,010 --> 00:51:20,035
that essentially do that
where they have like--

1055
00:51:20,035 --> 00:51:22,450
for those of you familiar
with hidden Markov models,

1056
00:51:22,450 --> 00:51:23,950
it's basically a
hidden Markov model

1057
00:51:23,950 --> 00:51:26,365
over whether someone has
mastered something or not.

1058
00:51:26,365 --> 00:51:28,240
Don't we have a different
type of state space

1059
00:51:28,240 --> 00:51:31,280
that they wrote down?

1060
00:51:31,280 --> 00:51:31,880
Yeah.

1061
00:51:31,880 --> 00:51:34,540
[INAUDIBLE] talked about-- we
basically wanted to [INAUDIBLE].

1062
00:51:34,540 --> 00:51:36,925
Oh, could you say your
name first, please?

1063
00:51:36,925 --> 00:51:39,550
The knowledge that the student
has and also maybe the questions

1064
00:51:39,550 --> 00:51:42,350
that have already been asked
to capture the environment,

1065
00:51:42,350 --> 00:51:44,470
the current environment
that we're at.

1066
00:51:44,470 --> 00:51:47,430
So I guess this is a better
representation of capturing

1067
00:51:47,430 --> 00:51:48,680
the knowledge the student has.

1068
00:51:48,680 --> 00:51:50,080
We were thinking of also just--

1069
00:51:50,080 --> 00:51:52,910
like the history of questions
and students' answers,

1070
00:51:52,910 --> 00:51:54,777
whether they got
it right or not.

1071
00:51:54,777 --> 00:51:56,860
I guess that's harder to
represent on [INAUDIBLE].

1072
00:51:56,860 --> 00:51:57,920
No, that's beautiful.

1073
00:51:57,920 --> 00:51:59,807
So exactly what
[AUDIO OUT] [INAUDIBLE].

1074
00:51:59,807 --> 00:52:01,390
So that was the other
one I was hoping

1075
00:52:01,390 --> 00:52:03,820
people might come up with,
which is the idea of this just

1076
00:52:03,820 --> 00:52:06,850
being a history, like a history
of all the previous questions

1077
00:52:06,850 --> 00:52:09,070
you've given or all the
questions the robot has

1078
00:52:09,070 --> 00:52:11,380
given the person and
what they've responded.

1079
00:52:11,380 --> 00:52:18,690
So you could imagine it's
like a observation question

1080
00:52:18,690 --> 00:52:21,960
reward dot, dot, dot.

1081
00:52:21,960 --> 00:52:24,000
And in fact, those
two representations

1082
00:52:24,000 --> 00:52:26,140
here, the history
and how the student--

1083
00:52:26,140 --> 00:52:28,050
how good the student,
can be, depending

1084
00:52:28,050 --> 00:52:30,360
on your representation,
be exactly isomorphic.

1085
00:52:30,360 --> 00:52:33,360
So sometimes this can be
a sufficient statistic

1086
00:52:33,360 --> 00:52:34,390
to capture that history.

1087
00:52:34,390 --> 00:52:35,890
And as [AUDIO OUT]
was pointing out,

1088
00:52:35,890 --> 00:52:37,390
one of the challenges
with histories

1089
00:52:37,390 --> 00:52:38,710
is that they grow unboundedly.

1090
00:52:38,710 --> 00:52:41,790
So if you want to have your
neural network be predicting

1091
00:52:41,790 --> 00:52:44,290
something, you might be able
to use something like an LSTM,

1092
00:52:44,290 --> 00:52:46,290
or you might want to
summarize the state.

1093
00:52:46,290 --> 00:52:49,105
So those are both great ideas
for what the states could be.

1094
00:52:49,105 --> 00:52:50,230
There's not a right answer.

1095
00:52:50,230 --> 00:52:51,110
Both of them would be great.

1096
00:52:51,110 --> 00:52:52,452
But there's also other ones.

1097
00:52:52,452 --> 00:52:54,910
The actions I heard many people
share what the actions are.

1098
00:52:54,910 --> 00:52:56,880
Someone want to tell me
what they are in there?

1099
00:52:56,880 --> 00:52:59,260
I know you guys mentioned
what the action space was.

1100
00:52:59,260 --> 00:52:59,760
Sure.

1101
00:52:59,760 --> 00:53:02,320
Just whether you pose in
addition or subtraction.

1102
00:53:02,320 --> 00:53:02,820
Exactly.

1103
00:53:02,820 --> 00:53:04,980
So these are just like
what the agent can actually

1104
00:53:04,980 --> 00:53:10,320
do, the teaching agent, addition
question or subtraction.

1105
00:53:10,320 --> 00:53:13,625
And the reward model is plus 1
if the student gets it right.

1106
00:53:17,310 --> 00:53:21,675
I saw some questions about
what a dynamics model is.

1107
00:53:21,675 --> 00:53:26,090
And inside of the responses,
people are putting on the form.

1108
00:53:26,090 --> 00:53:27,957
What I mean by a
dynamics model here--

1109
00:53:27,957 --> 00:53:29,540
and we'll talk a lot
more about this--

1110
00:53:29,540 --> 00:53:32,290
is what happens to the
state of the student

1111
00:53:32,290 --> 00:53:34,540
after a question is given.

1112
00:53:34,540 --> 00:53:37,720
So in this case-- and
I talked to some people

1113
00:53:37,720 --> 00:53:40,920
about this who had a great
understanding of this already.

1114
00:53:40,920 --> 00:53:42,670
The idea would be sort
of, how does either

1115
00:53:42,670 --> 00:53:46,720
that history change after you
give a question to the student,

1116
00:53:46,720 --> 00:53:49,150
or how does this the sort
of internal knowledge

1117
00:53:49,150 --> 00:53:50,420
of the student change?

1118
00:53:50,420 --> 00:53:53,170
So the hope would be as long
as this sort of curriculum

1119
00:53:53,170 --> 00:53:55,660
is vaguely reasonable, that
after you give the student

1120
00:53:55,660 --> 00:53:59,560
an addition question, they
now know more about addition,

1121
00:53:59,560 --> 00:54:02,140
or they're more likely to
have mastered addition.

1122
00:54:02,140 --> 00:54:05,350
So that would be sort of this
idea of there being a dynamics

1123
00:54:05,350 --> 00:54:09,190
process that where you start in
one state, you get an action,

1124
00:54:09,190 --> 00:54:11,890
and now you transition to
a new state afterwards.

1125
00:54:11,890 --> 00:54:14,230
And we'll talk a
lot more about that.

1126
00:54:14,230 --> 00:54:17,945
Now, what is the challenge with
this particular representation?

1127
00:54:17,945 --> 00:54:18,445
Yeah.

1128
00:54:18,445 --> 00:54:21,440
And can you say your
name first, please?

1129
00:54:21,440 --> 00:54:22,890
Depending on your
implementation,

1130
00:54:22,890 --> 00:54:27,300
there's a risk that the agent
just gives really easy problems.

1131
00:54:27,300 --> 00:54:27,800
Yeah.

1132
00:54:27,800 --> 00:54:29,760
In fact, [AUDIO OUT]
exactly right.

1133
00:54:29,760 --> 00:54:32,010
And in fact, that's exactly
what we think will happen.

1134
00:54:32,010 --> 00:54:39,580
So we think that an agent
that is maximizing its reward

1135
00:54:39,580 --> 00:54:45,820
should only give easy questions.

1136
00:54:45,820 --> 00:54:48,580
So in this paper, which
I took the inspiration

1137
00:54:48,580 --> 00:54:51,225
from for this example, it
was very close to this,

1138
00:54:51,225 --> 00:54:53,330
where they tried to
pick not correctness,

1139
00:54:53,330 --> 00:54:55,850
but how long it took
people to do problems.

1140
00:54:55,850 --> 00:54:59,080
And so if the student took
less time to do problems--

1141
00:54:59,080 --> 00:55:01,130
which isn't necessarily
bad in itself.

1142
00:55:01,130 --> 00:55:03,100
It might indicate some
notion of fluency--

1143
00:55:03,100 --> 00:55:04,550
the agent got more reward.

1144
00:55:04,550 --> 00:55:06,280
But, of course, what that
means is that you should just

1145
00:55:06,280 --> 00:55:08,500
give really easy questions that
will take the student no time

1146
00:55:08,500 --> 00:55:10,583
to do because then the
agent can get lots and lots

1147
00:55:10,583 --> 00:55:12,460
and lots of reward.

1148
00:55:12,460 --> 00:55:16,600
And this is probably not what
the intent-- like the designers

1149
00:55:16,600 --> 00:55:20,310
of this system to try to help
students learn things intended.

1150
00:55:20,310 --> 00:55:22,060
They probably actually
wanted the students

1151
00:55:22,060 --> 00:55:24,910
to learn both addition
and subtraction.

1152
00:55:24,910 --> 00:55:27,730
But I bring this up because this
is an example of what is often

1153
00:55:27,730 --> 00:55:31,960
called reward hacking, where
the reward that we specify

1154
00:55:31,960 --> 00:55:35,110
does not necessarily provide
the behavior that we really

1155
00:55:35,110 --> 00:55:36,400
hope to achieve.

1156
00:55:36,400 --> 00:55:38,775
And we will talk a
lot more about this.

1157
00:55:38,775 --> 00:55:40,940
In this case, it's a
fairly simple example

1158
00:55:40,940 --> 00:55:42,810
where we can see
it fairly quickly,

1159
00:55:42,810 --> 00:55:44,335
but there are a
lot of cases where

1160
00:55:44,335 --> 00:55:46,460
it's a lot more subtle to
understand whether or not

1161
00:55:46,460 --> 00:55:49,610
the system really will do
what you hope it will do.

1162
00:55:49,610 --> 00:55:53,660
And we'll talk about that
more throughout the course.

1163
00:55:53,660 --> 00:55:54,450
All right, great.

1164
00:55:54,450 --> 00:55:56,117
So we're going to now
just start to talk

1165
00:55:56,117 --> 00:55:58,530
about sort of sequential
decision-making more broadly.

1166
00:55:58,530 --> 00:56:00,180
And some of this will be
review for some of you,

1167
00:56:00,180 --> 00:56:02,222
but I think it's useful
to go through and refresh

1168
00:56:02,222 --> 00:56:03,680
our memories.

1169
00:56:03,680 --> 00:56:05,930
So the idea in sequential
decision-making

1170
00:56:05,930 --> 00:56:07,700
under uncertainty
is that we're going

1171
00:56:07,700 --> 00:56:11,880
to have an agent that is
taking decisions or actions.

1172
00:56:11,880 --> 00:56:16,370
So I'm going to use actions
and decisions interchangeably,

1173
00:56:16,370 --> 00:56:18,802
which are going to
interact in the world.

1174
00:56:18,802 --> 00:56:21,260
And then they're going to get
back some sort of observation

1175
00:56:21,260 --> 00:56:22,940
and reward signal.

1176
00:56:22,940 --> 00:56:25,170
So in the first example
I just gave you,

1177
00:56:25,170 --> 00:56:29,030
it's like the agent provides
a problem to the student.

1178
00:56:29,030 --> 00:56:30,800
And then they see
whether the student gets

1179
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:32,340
that correctly or incorrect.

1180
00:56:32,340 --> 00:56:35,720
And then they also use that
information to get a reward.

1181
00:56:35,720 --> 00:56:38,300
So it's giving
reward and feedback.

1182
00:56:38,300 --> 00:56:40,580
And the goal in this
case is for the agent

1183
00:56:40,580 --> 00:56:43,430
to select actions to maximize
the total expected future

1184
00:56:43,430 --> 00:56:46,410
reward, meaning both the
immediate reward they get now,

1185
00:56:46,410 --> 00:56:49,190
as well as the rewards they're
going to get over time.

1186
00:56:49,190 --> 00:56:50,600
And this generally
is often going

1187
00:56:50,600 --> 00:56:54,140
to involve balancing long-term
and short-term rewards.

1188
00:56:54,140 --> 00:56:55,980
So there are lots
and lots of examples.

1189
00:56:55,980 --> 00:56:58,890
I'll just go through a couple of
them just to give you a sense.

1190
00:56:58,890 --> 00:57:01,760
So one is something
like web advertising.

1191
00:57:01,760 --> 00:57:04,260
In this case,
Amazon, for example,

1192
00:57:04,260 --> 00:57:07,280
might choose like a web ad
to show you or a product

1193
00:57:07,280 --> 00:57:08,420
to suggest to you.

1194
00:57:08,420 --> 00:57:10,490
They might observe
things like view time

1195
00:57:10,490 --> 00:57:12,300
and whether or not
you click on the ad,

1196
00:57:12,300 --> 00:57:14,180
whether or not you
make a purchase.

1197
00:57:14,180 --> 00:57:15,950
And the goal in this
case could probably

1198
00:57:15,950 --> 00:57:18,350
be for them to optimize
either click time or view

1199
00:57:18,350 --> 00:57:21,290
time or revenue.

1200
00:57:21,290 --> 00:57:23,820
In the context of
something like robotics,

1201
00:57:23,820 --> 00:57:26,360
the control space or
the decision space

1202
00:57:26,360 --> 00:57:29,510
might be something like
how to move a joint.

1203
00:57:29,510 --> 00:57:32,150
And then the feedback that
the robot might get back

1204
00:57:32,150 --> 00:57:34,370
might be something like a
camera image of a kitchen.

1205
00:57:34,370 --> 00:57:35,960
And perhaps they
just get a plus 1

1206
00:57:35,960 --> 00:57:38,840
if there are no more
dishes on the counter.

1207
00:57:38,840 --> 00:57:41,000
Now, just a quick question,
could this potentially

1208
00:57:41,000 --> 00:57:43,910
be a reward-hacked
specification?

1209
00:57:43,910 --> 00:57:44,820
I see some smiles.

1210
00:57:44,820 --> 00:57:47,520
What could happen?

1211
00:57:47,520 --> 00:57:48,020
Yeah.

1212
00:57:48,020 --> 00:57:48,980
[INAUDIBLE]

1213
00:57:48,980 --> 00:57:50,030
Oh, sorry.

1214
00:57:50,030 --> 00:57:53,300
Robot could just push
everything off the counter.

1215
00:57:53,300 --> 00:57:55,475
Which I will say with--

1216
00:57:55,475 --> 00:57:56,577
it's tempting, right?

1217
00:57:56,577 --> 00:57:58,410
Like, I'm just going
to make it all go away.

1218
00:57:58,410 --> 00:58:00,180
But in fact, this does
not solve the problem.

1219
00:58:00,180 --> 00:58:02,480
And now you just have broken
dishes and food on the floor.

1220
00:58:02,480 --> 00:58:04,020
So that would not be
a good thing to do.

1221
00:58:04,020 --> 00:58:06,530
So yeah, this would be probably
not a great reward to put.

1222
00:58:06,530 --> 00:58:08,863
You probably want a reward
more like that the dishes are

1223
00:58:08,863 --> 00:58:10,993
inside of the dishwasher
and finally clean.

1224
00:58:10,993 --> 00:58:12,660
So not just that they
were put in there,

1225
00:58:12,660 --> 00:58:14,730
but actually that you
ran the dishwasher.

1226
00:58:14,730 --> 00:58:18,290
So this would be a second
example of a setting.

1227
00:58:18,290 --> 00:58:21,200
Another would be something like
blood pressure control, where

1228
00:58:21,200 --> 00:58:24,170
you could imagine that the
agent gives recommendations

1229
00:58:24,170 --> 00:58:26,190
like exercise or medication.

1230
00:58:26,190 --> 00:58:28,470
The feedback is things
like blood pressure.

1231
00:58:28,470 --> 00:58:30,710
And then you would define
some reward like maybe

1232
00:58:30,710 --> 00:58:33,290
plus 1 if you're in a
healthy range, else some sort

1233
00:58:33,290 --> 00:58:36,145
of sloping penalty for being
outside of the healthy range.

1234
00:58:39,420 --> 00:58:39,920
All right.

1235
00:58:39,920 --> 00:58:41,295
So all of these
are nice examples

1236
00:58:41,295 --> 00:58:42,910
of the numerous
ways where we often

1237
00:58:42,910 --> 00:58:45,630
try to make sequences of
decisions under uncertainty.

1238
00:58:45,630 --> 00:58:48,520
In general, we're going
to assume that we have

1239
00:58:48,520 --> 00:58:50,578
a finite series of time steps.

1240
00:58:50,578 --> 00:58:52,870
So we're not going to be
thinking about continuous time

1241
00:58:52,870 --> 00:58:53,575
in this class.

1242
00:58:53,575 --> 00:58:54,950
Lots of interesting
things there.

1243
00:58:54,950 --> 00:58:55,970
We're not going to cover it.

1244
00:58:55,970 --> 00:58:57,928
What we're going to assume
is that the agent is

1245
00:58:57,928 --> 00:58:59,140
making a series of decisions.

1246
00:58:59,140 --> 00:59:01,682
So we're going to think of there
being a series of time steps

1247
00:59:01,682 --> 00:59:04,760
like 1 minute, 2 minutes,
3 minute, 4 minute.

1248
00:59:04,760 --> 00:59:06,290
The agent will take an action.

1249
00:59:06,290 --> 00:59:08,530
The world will update
given that action and emit

1250
00:59:08,530 --> 00:59:10,430
an observation and a reward.

1251
00:59:10,430 --> 00:59:12,850
And then the agent
receives that, updates,

1252
00:59:12,850 --> 00:59:14,410
and then makes another decision.

1253
00:59:14,410 --> 00:59:15,590
We just close this loop.

1254
00:59:15,590 --> 00:59:18,070
It's a feedback cycle.

1255
00:59:18,070 --> 00:59:21,650
In this case, as we sort of just
talked about at a high level,

1256
00:59:21,650 --> 00:59:23,270
we can think of there
being histories,

1257
00:59:23,270 --> 00:59:26,590
which is sequences of
past actions, rewards,

1258
00:59:26,590 --> 00:59:30,010
and observations up to
the present time point.

1259
00:59:30,010 --> 00:59:32,350
So the history,
ht, would consist

1260
00:59:32,350 --> 00:59:34,550
of all the previous
actions of the agent,

1261
00:59:34,550 --> 00:59:38,200
the observations it receives,
and the reward it's got.

1262
00:59:38,200 --> 00:59:41,375
In general, this is something
you could use to make decisions.

1263
00:59:41,375 --> 00:59:43,000
You could just keep
track of everything

1264
00:59:43,000 --> 00:59:44,740
you've experienced
so far and then

1265
00:59:44,740 --> 00:59:47,950
condition on that to try
to make your next decision.

1266
00:59:47,950 --> 00:59:50,110
But we often are going
to assume that there's

1267
00:59:50,110 --> 00:59:52,300
some sort of
sufficient statistic

1268
00:59:52,300 --> 00:59:54,880
that we can use to
summarize the history.

1269
00:59:54,880 --> 00:59:57,280
It will be much more
practical in many cases.

1270
00:59:57,280 --> 00:59:57,780
Yeah.

1271
00:59:57,780 --> 00:59:58,420
Oh, sorry.

1272
00:59:58,420 --> 01:00:01,270
Just [INAUDIBLE] observation
is basically like the history,

1273
01:00:01,270 --> 01:00:03,795
like a previous history
of the [INAUDIBLE]

1274
01:00:03,795 --> 01:00:04,670
And what's your name?

1275
01:00:04,670 --> 01:00:06,700
[AUDIO OUT]

1276
01:00:06,700 --> 01:00:08,260
So the observation
in this case would

1277
01:00:08,260 --> 01:00:10,720
be something like the immediate
information you get back

1278
01:00:10,720 --> 01:00:11,780
after the last action.

1279
01:00:11,780 --> 01:00:13,410
So in the case of
the student, it

1280
01:00:13,410 --> 01:00:16,118
would have been whether they get
the last problem correct or not.

1281
01:00:16,118 --> 01:00:17,560
So just like a single time step.

1282
01:00:17,560 --> 01:00:20,230
And then the history
would be everything

1283
01:00:20,230 --> 01:00:21,722
like up to this time point.

1284
01:00:21,722 --> 01:00:22,305
Good question.

1285
01:00:25,060 --> 01:00:27,560
So in particular, often
to make things tractable

1286
01:00:27,560 --> 01:00:30,160
and because often, in reality,
it's not a terrible assumption,

1287
01:00:30,160 --> 01:00:33,380
we're going to normally
make the Markov assumption.

1288
01:00:33,380 --> 01:00:35,650
And the idea is that
we're going to try

1289
01:00:35,650 --> 01:00:37,900
to come up with some sort
of informative information

1290
01:00:37,900 --> 01:00:40,430
state that is a sufficient
statistic of the history.

1291
01:00:40,430 --> 01:00:42,100
So we don't have
to keep around all

1292
01:00:42,100 --> 01:00:43,900
of the prior history
of everything

1293
01:00:43,900 --> 01:00:47,620
the agent's ever done or
seen or gotten a reward for.

1294
01:00:47,620 --> 01:00:50,710
And what we say is a
state, St, is Markov if

1295
01:00:50,710 --> 01:00:55,060
and only if the probability of
going to the next state given

1296
01:00:55,060 --> 01:00:58,510
the current state in action is
the same as if you conditioned

1297
01:00:58,510 --> 01:01:01,420
on the whole entire history.

1298
01:01:01,420 --> 01:01:03,340
So another way to say
this, which I think

1299
01:01:03,340 --> 01:01:05,770
is kind of a nice
evocative idea--

1300
01:01:05,770 --> 01:01:07,540
this is not from me,
this is from others--

1301
01:01:07,540 --> 01:01:09,850
is that the future is
independent of the past, given

1302
01:01:09,850 --> 01:01:11,950
the present.

1303
01:01:11,950 --> 01:01:14,458
That means if you have
a rich representation

1304
01:01:14,458 --> 01:01:16,000
of your current
state, you don't have

1305
01:01:16,000 --> 01:01:19,240
to think about the
previous history.

1306
01:01:19,240 --> 01:01:21,610
And, of course, in
general, this will be true

1307
01:01:21,610 --> 01:01:25,310
if you make S equal to ht.

1308
01:01:25,310 --> 01:01:27,310
But in general, we're
going to be thinking often

1309
01:01:27,310 --> 01:01:30,050
of sort of projecting down to
a much smaller state space.

1310
01:01:30,050 --> 01:01:31,740
So for example, you
might say, well, I

1311
01:01:31,740 --> 01:01:34,490
could think about someone's
blood pressure from all of time,

1312
01:01:34,490 --> 01:01:37,115
but maybe it's sufficient just
to think of their blood pressure

1313
01:01:37,115 --> 01:01:40,690
over the last like two hours in
order to make my next decision.

1314
01:01:40,690 --> 01:01:42,784
Yeah.

1315
01:01:42,784 --> 01:01:44,250
Uh-huh.

1316
01:01:44,250 --> 01:01:47,520
Is there a difference
between state and observation

1317
01:01:47,520 --> 01:01:48,945
in this case?

1318
01:01:48,945 --> 01:01:49,570
Great question.

1319
01:01:49,570 --> 01:01:50,267
Yes, in general.

1320
01:01:50,267 --> 01:01:51,850
So I'll give you a
particular example.

1321
01:01:51,850 --> 01:01:55,230
Atari, which is these
video games that DeepMind

1322
01:01:55,230 --> 01:01:57,960
learned an agent to play,
what their state in that case

1323
01:01:57,960 --> 01:02:00,360
was the last four frames.

1324
01:02:00,360 --> 01:02:02,530
So not just the last frame,
the last four frames.

1325
01:02:02,530 --> 01:02:03,840
Does anybody have any
idea why you might want

1326
01:02:03,840 --> 01:02:05,370
four frames instead of one?

1327
01:02:05,370 --> 01:02:06,720
Yeah.

1328
01:02:06,720 --> 01:02:10,050
Maybe like-- so you can
see if there's momentum

1329
01:02:10,050 --> 01:02:11,290
to an object already moving.

1330
01:02:11,290 --> 01:02:11,790
Exactly.

1331
01:02:11,790 --> 01:02:13,740
It gives you velocity
and acceleration.

1332
01:02:13,740 --> 01:02:14,430
Yeah.

1333
01:02:14,430 --> 01:02:16,050
So there are a
number of cases where

1334
01:02:16,050 --> 01:02:17,880
you might think that there are
parts of the state that really

1335
01:02:17,880 --> 01:02:19,210
depend on temporal differences.

1336
01:02:19,210 --> 01:02:20,752
And then in those
cases, you're going

1337
01:02:20,752 --> 01:02:23,170
to want more than just
the immediate state.

1338
01:02:23,170 --> 01:02:23,670
Great.

1339
01:02:23,670 --> 01:02:26,120
Great questions.

1340
01:02:26,120 --> 01:02:26,620
All right.

1341
01:02:26,620 --> 01:02:27,930
So why is this popular?

1342
01:02:27,930 --> 01:02:30,060
It's used all the time.

1343
01:02:30,060 --> 01:02:30,910
It's simple.

1344
01:02:30,910 --> 01:02:32,140
It can often be satisfied.

1345
01:02:32,140 --> 01:02:34,557
As we were just discussing if
you use some history as part

1346
01:02:34,557 --> 01:02:35,760
of the state.

1347
01:02:35,760 --> 01:02:38,070
Generally, there are many
cases where you can just

1348
01:02:38,070 --> 01:02:39,610
use the most recent state.

1349
01:02:39,610 --> 01:02:41,680
Not always, but many cases.

1350
01:02:41,680 --> 01:02:44,680
And it has huge implications
for computational complexity

1351
01:02:44,680 --> 01:02:47,393
data required in the
resulting performance.

1352
01:02:47,393 --> 01:02:49,060
What I mean by the
resulting performance

1353
01:02:49,060 --> 01:02:51,200
is is that in many
of these cases,

1354
01:02:51,200 --> 01:02:53,450
just like in a lot of
statistics and machine learning,

1355
01:02:53,450 --> 01:02:56,170
there will be trade-offs
between bias and variance.

1356
01:02:56,170 --> 01:02:58,960
And so there'll be a trade-off
between using states that

1357
01:02:58,960 --> 01:03:01,540
are really small and
easy for us to work with

1358
01:03:01,540 --> 01:03:03,190
but aren't really
able to capture

1359
01:03:03,190 --> 01:03:05,230
the complexity of the
world and the applications

1360
01:03:05,230 --> 01:03:07,000
we care about so
that it might be

1361
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:09,620
fast to learn with those
sort of representations.

1362
01:03:09,620 --> 01:03:11,770
But ultimately,
performance is poor.

1363
01:03:11,770 --> 01:03:13,240
So there will
often be trade-offs

1364
01:03:13,240 --> 01:03:15,070
with how we actually--
the expressive

1365
01:03:15,070 --> 01:03:17,410
power of our representations
versus how long it

1366
01:03:17,410 --> 01:03:18,195
takes us to learn.

1367
01:03:21,450 --> 01:03:21,950
Right.

1368
01:03:21,950 --> 01:03:23,408
So one of the big
questions when we

1369
01:03:23,408 --> 01:03:25,580
talk about sequential
decision-making processes

1370
01:03:25,580 --> 01:03:28,260
is, is the state Markov?

1371
01:03:28,260 --> 01:03:31,010
And is the world
partially observable?

1372
01:03:31,010 --> 01:03:32,620
So partial-- oh, yeah.

1373
01:03:35,590 --> 01:03:38,410
My question is that, doesn't
the Markov assumption

1374
01:03:38,410 --> 01:03:43,385
make this reward attribution
problem somehow harder?

1375
01:03:43,385 --> 01:03:44,180
All right.

1376
01:03:44,180 --> 01:03:45,038
Good question.

1377
01:03:45,038 --> 01:03:45,830
Well, I don't know.

1378
01:03:45,830 --> 01:03:48,330
I guess you could imagine it
might make it easier or harder.

1379
01:03:48,330 --> 01:03:50,695
There's still the
question of you

1380
01:03:50,695 --> 01:03:52,637
might only get periodic rewards.

1381
01:03:52,637 --> 01:03:54,220
And you still would
have to figure out

1382
01:03:54,220 --> 01:03:56,590
which decisions caused
you to get to a state

1383
01:03:56,590 --> 01:03:59,300
where you got those rewards.

1384
01:03:59,300 --> 01:04:00,020
[INAUDIBLE]

1385
01:04:00,020 --> 01:04:00,600
Yeah.

1386
01:04:00,600 --> 01:04:02,100
So let me think of
it-- so you might

1387
01:04:02,100 --> 01:04:03,860
have a case where
the reward might

1388
01:04:03,860 --> 01:04:07,190
be a function of
your current state.

1389
01:04:07,190 --> 01:04:08,360
Yeah.

1390
01:04:08,360 --> 01:04:11,350
Let me think if I can't
think of a good example.

1391
01:04:11,350 --> 01:04:17,300
OK, so let's say maybe you
want to run a marathon.

1392
01:04:17,300 --> 01:04:19,570
And you get a plus
100 if you make it.

1393
01:04:19,570 --> 01:04:21,820
Boston Marathon is a competitive
marathon to get into,

1394
01:04:21,820 --> 01:04:25,028
so you get a plus 100 if
you can qualify for Boston.

1395
01:04:25,028 --> 01:04:27,570
And you do a lot of different
things in your training regime.

1396
01:04:27,570 --> 01:04:31,610
You eat healthy, and you
sleep, and you train.

1397
01:04:31,610 --> 01:04:33,690
And you get zero
reward for any of that.

1398
01:04:33,690 --> 01:04:35,790
And then on the
day of your race,

1399
01:04:35,790 --> 01:04:37,760
you see if you
qualify for Boston.

1400
01:04:37,760 --> 01:04:39,860
So your state only--

1401
01:04:39,860 --> 01:04:41,870
like your reward for
getting into Boston

1402
01:04:41,870 --> 01:04:43,440
only depends on
that current state.

1403
01:04:43,440 --> 01:04:45,273
But you don't know which
of those decisions.

1404
01:04:45,273 --> 01:04:46,380
Was it that you ate well?

1405
01:04:46,380 --> 01:04:47,220
Was it that you slept?

1406
01:04:47,220 --> 01:04:48,637
Was it that you
trained every week

1407
01:04:48,637 --> 01:04:52,280
for 17 weeks caused you to
get to the state in which you

1408
01:04:52,280 --> 01:04:54,080
qualified for Boston?

1409
01:04:54,080 --> 01:04:56,970
And so that's independent of the
Markov assumption in that case,

1410
01:04:56,970 --> 01:04:59,190
because you still have
the question of what

1411
01:04:59,190 --> 01:05:02,310
series of decisions allowed
you to get to a state that

1412
01:05:02,310 --> 01:05:04,050
achieved high reward.

1413
01:05:04,050 --> 01:05:06,360
Great question.

1414
01:05:06,360 --> 01:05:08,860
So another thing is where the
world is partially observable.

1415
01:05:08,860 --> 01:05:11,200
We will mostly not be talking
about this in this class.

1416
01:05:11,200 --> 01:05:13,230
Mykel Kochenderfer
has a great class

1417
01:05:13,230 --> 01:05:15,270
where he talks about
this a lot, but this

1418
01:05:15,270 --> 01:05:18,360
does relate to the case we
talked about with students.

1419
01:05:18,360 --> 01:05:20,297
So for students,
one way you could

1420
01:05:20,297 --> 01:05:22,380
think about that is that
there's some latent state

1421
01:05:22,380 --> 01:05:23,940
that you can't
directly access, which

1422
01:05:23,940 --> 01:05:25,440
is whether or not
they know addition

1423
01:05:25,440 --> 01:05:26,770
or they know subtraction.

1424
01:05:26,770 --> 01:05:28,530
But you get noisy
observations when

1425
01:05:28,530 --> 01:05:31,680
they do problems where they
get it right or get it wrong.

1426
01:05:31,680 --> 01:05:34,200
And the reason it's noisy
is because all of us

1427
01:05:34,200 --> 01:05:35,700
make mistakes on
addition sometimes,

1428
01:05:35,700 --> 01:05:38,075
whereas I have complete faith
that everyone here actually

1429
01:05:38,075 --> 01:05:39,390
knows how to do addition.

1430
01:05:39,390 --> 01:05:42,190
And sometimes you might guess
right even if you don't know it.

1431
01:05:42,190 --> 01:05:43,600
So the idea is that it's latent.

1432
01:05:43,600 --> 01:05:45,690
You don't directly
get to observe it.

1433
01:05:45,690 --> 01:05:49,390
This comes up in a lot of
robotics problems, too,

1434
01:05:49,390 --> 01:05:51,490
so I'll just give a
quick example here.

1435
01:05:51,490 --> 01:05:55,980
If you have a robot that uses a
laser rangefinder to figure out

1436
01:05:55,980 --> 01:06:02,310
these little arrows or lasers to
figure out its environment-- so

1437
01:06:02,310 --> 01:06:04,583
it could have 180 degrees
of laser rangefinders.

1438
01:06:04,583 --> 01:06:06,000
And what it's
getting back is just

1439
01:06:06,000 --> 01:06:08,490
the distance in all of these
different angles to where

1440
01:06:08,490 --> 01:06:09,870
it hits a wall.

1441
01:06:09,870 --> 01:06:13,200
So as you can imagine, many
rooms would look identical.

1442
01:06:13,200 --> 01:06:15,480
So any room that has like
kind of the same dimensions

1443
01:06:15,480 --> 01:06:17,088
would look identical
to that robot.

1444
01:06:17,088 --> 01:06:18,630
And it wouldn't be
able to tell is it

1445
01:06:18,630 --> 01:06:20,640
on the third floor
or the second floor.

1446
01:06:20,640 --> 01:06:22,590
So that would be a
partially observable case

1447
01:06:22,590 --> 01:06:24,720
where it can't uniquely
identify its state based

1448
01:06:24,720 --> 01:06:26,640
on its observations.

1449
01:06:26,640 --> 01:06:28,240
So we won't talk
too much about that,

1450
01:06:28,240 --> 01:06:29,530
but it's important
to know about.

1451
01:06:29,530 --> 01:06:31,238
Another thing is
whether the dynamics are

1452
01:06:31,238 --> 01:06:33,060
deterministic or stochastic.

1453
01:06:33,060 --> 01:06:34,860
So there are many
cases where things

1454
01:06:34,860 --> 01:06:36,460
are close to deterministic.

1455
01:06:36,460 --> 01:06:41,400
Like, if I put down a piece
on a Go board, it goes there.

1456
01:06:41,400 --> 01:06:44,140
But there are other things that
we often treat as stochastic.

1457
01:06:44,140 --> 01:06:45,690
Like, when I flip
a coin, I don't

1458
01:06:45,690 --> 01:06:47,610
know whether it's going
to be heads or tails.

1459
01:06:47,610 --> 01:06:49,462
So that will be an
important decision.

1460
01:06:49,462 --> 01:06:51,420
And then the final thing
is whether the actions

1461
01:06:51,420 --> 01:06:57,640
influence only immediate
reward or reward in next state.

1462
01:06:57,640 --> 01:06:59,350
So as an example
of this, you might

1463
01:06:59,350 --> 01:07:01,450
imagine if you were
making a policy for what

1464
01:07:01,450 --> 01:07:02,770
ad to show to people.

1465
01:07:02,770 --> 01:07:05,420
And you just imagine for each
person coming onto the web,

1466
01:07:05,420 --> 01:07:06,640
you just show them--

1467
01:07:06,640 --> 01:07:09,040
onto your website, you show
an ad, and then they go away.

1468
01:07:09,040 --> 01:07:11,560
And they either buy
something, or they don't.

1469
01:07:11,560 --> 01:07:14,900
A bandit would be a case where
you just have-- bless you.

1470
01:07:14,900 --> 01:07:16,670
You have a series of
customers coming in.

1471
01:07:16,670 --> 01:07:21,610
And so whether or not
I show a particular ad

1472
01:07:21,610 --> 01:07:24,670
and he clicks on it or does
not impact whether or not Ellen

1473
01:07:24,670 --> 01:07:27,550
comes along and likes an ad.

1474
01:07:27,550 --> 01:07:30,530
So that's a case where it
impacts your immediate reward,

1475
01:07:30,530 --> 01:07:32,350
but not the next state.

1476
01:07:32,350 --> 01:07:34,530
We can talk more about that.

1477
01:07:34,530 --> 01:07:35,030
All right.

1478
01:07:35,030 --> 01:07:37,460
Let's think about a particular
sort of running example.

1479
01:07:37,460 --> 01:07:38,930
We'll think of a Mars rover.

1480
01:07:38,930 --> 01:07:41,440
So Mars rover is a
Markov decision process.

1481
01:07:41,440 --> 01:07:43,040
Imagine that Mars
is really small.

1482
01:07:43,040 --> 01:07:45,100
We only have seven
places in Mars.

1483
01:07:45,100 --> 01:07:48,040
So in this case, we
would have the state

1484
01:07:48,040 --> 01:07:50,650
is the location of the
Rover, which is one

1485
01:07:50,650 --> 01:07:52,790
of seven discrete locations.

1486
01:07:52,790 --> 01:07:54,520
We could have actions
called try left

1487
01:07:54,520 --> 01:07:57,240
and try right, meaning that
our Rover is not perfect.

1488
01:07:57,240 --> 01:07:59,240
So sometimes it tries
to go a direction,

1489
01:07:59,240 --> 01:08:00,650
and it doesn't succeed.

1490
01:08:00,650 --> 01:08:02,550
And let's imagine
that we have rewards,

1491
01:08:02,550 --> 01:08:05,010
which is that there's some
interesting field sites.

1492
01:08:05,010 --> 01:08:08,390
And so if you spend time
over here, you get a plus 1.

1493
01:08:08,390 --> 01:08:10,670
And you have spent over
here, you get a plus 10.

1494
01:08:10,670 --> 01:08:12,930
And else, you get zero reward.

1495
01:08:12,930 --> 01:08:14,350
So this would be
a particular case

1496
01:08:14,350 --> 01:08:16,350
where we could think of
there being these states

1497
01:08:16,350 --> 01:08:18,664
and these actions and rewards.

1498
01:08:21,990 --> 01:08:25,240
So when we think of a
Markov decision process,

1499
01:08:25,240 --> 01:08:28,890
we think of there being a
dynamics and a reward model.

1500
01:08:28,890 --> 01:08:32,490
So in particular,
the dynamics model

1501
01:08:32,490 --> 01:08:35,069
is going to tell us
how the state evolves

1502
01:08:35,069 --> 01:08:36,529
as we make decisions.

1503
01:08:36,529 --> 01:08:38,770
We will not always have
direct access to this,

1504
01:08:38,770 --> 01:08:40,590
but the idea is
that in the world,

1505
01:08:40,590 --> 01:08:42,960
there is some dynamics
process and things are

1506
01:08:42,960 --> 01:08:45,210
changing as we make decisions.

1507
01:08:45,210 --> 01:08:48,029
So in particular,
we generally want

1508
01:08:48,029 --> 01:08:50,670
to allow for stochastic
systems, meaning that

1509
01:08:50,670 --> 01:08:53,950
given we're currently in a state
and we take a particular action,

1510
01:08:53,950 --> 01:08:57,630
what is the distribution over
next states that we might reach?

1511
01:08:57,630 --> 01:08:59,580
So for example, I'm
that Mars rover,

1512
01:08:59,580 --> 01:09:02,590
and I'm going to try
to go to the right.

1513
01:09:02,590 --> 01:09:06,819
It might be that I can go to
the right with 50% probability,

1514
01:09:06,819 --> 01:09:08,800
but I'm not a very
accurate Rover.

1515
01:09:08,800 --> 01:09:10,920
And so 50% of the
time I go to the left,

1516
01:09:10,920 --> 01:09:13,500
or maybe I stay in
the same location.

1517
01:09:13,500 --> 01:09:16,135
So this dynamics model just
specifies what actually

1518
01:09:16,135 --> 01:09:18,510
the distribution of outcomes
that can happen in the world

1519
01:09:18,510 --> 01:09:20,700
when I make a decision.

1520
01:09:20,700 --> 01:09:23,720
The reward model predicts
the immediate reward,

1521
01:09:23,720 --> 01:09:26,180
which is if I'm in this
state and I take this action,

1522
01:09:26,180 --> 01:09:28,569
what is my expected reward?

1523
01:09:28,569 --> 01:09:31,939
I want to highlight here that
there are different conventions.

1524
01:09:31,939 --> 01:09:34,149
You could have the
reward be a function only

1525
01:09:34,149 --> 01:09:35,470
of the current state--

1526
01:09:35,470 --> 01:09:36,319
excuse me.

1527
01:09:36,319 --> 01:09:39,050
It could be a function of the
state and the action you take,

1528
01:09:39,050 --> 01:09:41,550
or it could be a function of
the state, the action you take,

1529
01:09:41,550 --> 01:09:43,649
and the next state you reach.

1530
01:09:43,649 --> 01:09:46,529
You'll see all of these
conventions in reinforcement

1531
01:09:46,529 --> 01:09:47,700
learning papers.

1532
01:09:47,700 --> 01:09:50,109
Probably the most
common one is this.

1533
01:09:50,109 --> 01:09:52,170
But we'll try just to be
specific whenever we're

1534
01:09:52,170 --> 01:09:54,142
using it so that it's clear.

1535
01:09:54,142 --> 01:09:56,100
And you can always ask
me or ask any of the TAs

1536
01:09:56,100 --> 01:09:58,430
if it's not clear.

1537
01:09:58,430 --> 01:09:58,930
Bless you.

1538
01:09:58,930 --> 01:10:02,320
So let's think about sort of
what a stochastic Mars rover

1539
01:10:02,320 --> 01:10:03,350
model would be.

1540
01:10:03,350 --> 01:10:06,760
So I've written down
a particular choice

1541
01:10:06,760 --> 01:10:07,860
for the reward.

1542
01:10:07,860 --> 01:10:11,620
And let's imagine that
part of the dynamics model

1543
01:10:11,620 --> 01:10:14,740
is the following, which
is if I start in state S1

1544
01:10:14,740 --> 01:10:17,230
and I try to go
to the right, then

1545
01:10:17,230 --> 01:10:20,320
I have some probability
of going to S2,

1546
01:10:20,320 --> 01:10:24,610
else I have some
probability of staying here.

1547
01:10:24,610 --> 01:10:26,330
What I want to be
clear about here--

1548
01:10:26,330 --> 01:10:29,570
and this relates to the
question before about models--

1549
01:10:29,570 --> 01:10:33,590
is that this is like the agent's
idea of how the world works.

1550
01:10:33,590 --> 01:10:36,100
It doesn't have to be how
the world actually works.

1551
01:10:36,100 --> 01:10:38,470
So what I told you in
the previous slides

1552
01:10:38,470 --> 01:10:42,190
is that imagine in this
world, in reality, this

1553
01:10:42,190 --> 01:10:43,720
gives you plus 1,
and this gives you

1554
01:10:43,720 --> 01:10:45,700
plus 10 in terms of the reward.

1555
01:10:45,700 --> 01:10:47,600
That's how the world
actually works.

1556
01:10:47,600 --> 01:10:49,690
But the agent might
have the wrong model

1557
01:10:49,690 --> 01:10:52,300
of how the world works because
it only learns about the world

1558
01:10:52,300 --> 01:10:55,840
through its experiences, or it
just might have a bad model.

1559
01:10:55,840 --> 01:10:59,660
So this is an example of sort
of like a model-based Markov

1560
01:10:59,660 --> 01:11:03,500
system where the agent would
have a particular representation

1561
01:11:03,500 --> 01:11:06,230
of the dynamics model and a
particular assumption over how

1562
01:11:06,230 --> 01:11:07,105
the rewards work.

1563
01:11:11,030 --> 01:11:13,402
In these settings,
we have a policy.

1564
01:11:13,402 --> 01:11:15,860
A decision policy is just going
to be a mapping from states

1565
01:11:15,860 --> 01:11:17,450
to actions.

1566
01:11:17,450 --> 01:11:19,850
It's like an if then table.

1567
01:11:19,850 --> 01:11:22,850
If it's deterministic, we
just have a single action

1568
01:11:22,850 --> 01:11:25,340
that we would take in
a particular state.

1569
01:11:25,340 --> 01:11:29,660
Like, maybe we always show this
one ad to a particular customer.

1570
01:11:29,660 --> 01:11:33,442
Or we could have a stochastic
policy where we randomize this.

1571
01:11:33,442 --> 01:11:36,150
So this would be something like,
oh, when this customer shows up,

1572
01:11:36,150 --> 01:11:39,470
I show a vacation ad,
or I show a board game

1573
01:11:39,470 --> 01:11:43,430
ad with 90%
probability versus 10%.

1574
01:11:43,430 --> 01:11:45,630
Both types of policies
are really common,

1575
01:11:45,630 --> 01:11:47,960
and it can depend in part
what sort of domain you're in

1576
01:11:47,960 --> 01:11:52,730
and whether you're trying to
learn from that experience.

1577
01:11:52,730 --> 01:11:55,170
OK, so let's see what that
would look like in this case.

1578
01:11:55,170 --> 01:11:59,070
So for Mars rover, you could
say that no matter where it is,

1579
01:11:59,070 --> 01:12:00,560
it always just
tries to go right.

1580
01:12:04,080 --> 01:12:06,000
So that would just
be one example

1581
01:12:06,000 --> 01:12:07,650
of a policy you could have.

1582
01:12:07,650 --> 01:12:10,380
And it just requires you to
specify for every single state

1583
01:12:10,380 --> 01:12:11,910
what is the action
you would take

1584
01:12:11,910 --> 01:12:14,255
or what is the distribution
over actions you would take.

1585
01:12:17,350 --> 01:12:18,880
So in this sort
of setting, we're

1586
01:12:18,880 --> 01:12:20,715
normally interested in
two main-- oh, yeah.

1587
01:12:20,715 --> 01:12:21,340
Yeah, question.

1588
01:12:21,340 --> 01:12:26,290
So it's like making decisions
based on the state that it's in.

1589
01:12:26,290 --> 01:12:31,220
And it learn to switch from
different types of policies.

1590
01:12:31,220 --> 01:12:33,680
So not just different actions
for some of the state,

1591
01:12:33,680 --> 01:12:37,540
but also switch to checking the
past state, the future state.

1592
01:12:37,540 --> 01:12:39,130
In the same way like
in deep learning,

1593
01:12:39,130 --> 01:12:41,420
it tries a bunch of
different functions.

1594
01:12:41,420 --> 01:12:43,570
Can it do that, or
can it not do that?

1595
01:12:43,570 --> 01:12:44,070
Great.

1596
01:12:44,070 --> 01:12:45,858
And remind me your name.

1597
01:12:45,858 --> 01:12:47,650
[AUDIO OUT]

1598
01:12:47,650 --> 01:12:48,230
Yeah.

1599
01:12:48,230 --> 01:12:48,980
So great question.

1600
01:12:48,980 --> 01:12:49,792
It will in general.

1601
01:12:49,792 --> 01:12:51,250
In general, when
we're learning, it

1602
01:12:51,250 --> 01:12:53,418
will change its policy
a lot over time.

1603
01:12:53,418 --> 01:12:55,210
So it might start with
a particular policy.

1604
01:12:55,210 --> 01:12:56,668
And then over time,
it will explore

1605
01:12:56,668 --> 01:12:58,780
lots of different policies
in trying to search

1606
01:12:58,780 --> 01:13:00,285
for something that's good.

1607
01:13:00,285 --> 01:13:01,660
That's a great
question, and that

1608
01:13:01,660 --> 01:13:03,380
relates to what I was
just putting here,

1609
01:13:03,380 --> 01:13:05,200
which is two of the
central questions we're

1610
01:13:05,200 --> 01:13:07,575
going to talk a lot about,
particularly at the beginning,

1611
01:13:07,575 --> 01:13:09,340
is evaluation and control.

1612
01:13:09,340 --> 01:13:12,650
Evaluation says someone
gives you a fixed policy.

1613
01:13:12,650 --> 01:13:14,210
And you want to
know how good it is.

1614
01:13:14,210 --> 01:13:15,850
Like, maybe your
boss says, hey, I

1615
01:13:15,850 --> 01:13:18,100
think this is the right way
to advertise to customers,

1616
01:13:18,100 --> 01:13:19,725
and we're going to
make a lot of money.

1617
01:13:19,725 --> 01:13:22,490
And you go out, and you just
deploy that particular decision

1618
01:13:22,490 --> 01:13:22,990
policy.

1619
01:13:22,990 --> 01:13:24,820
And you see how
much money you make.

1620
01:13:24,820 --> 01:13:26,710
So that would be evaluation.

1621
01:13:26,710 --> 01:13:29,560
Control is you actually want
to find the best policy.

1622
01:13:29,560 --> 01:13:32,180
And so in general, to
actually find the best policy,

1623
01:13:32,180 --> 01:13:34,700
we're going to have to do
a lot of trial and error.

1624
01:13:34,700 --> 01:13:36,950
And we want to do that in
a strategic, efficient way

1625
01:13:36,950 --> 01:13:39,285
so we can quickly learn
what that good policy is.

1626
01:13:42,140 --> 01:13:44,460
So in general, we're going
to be talking about things.

1627
01:13:44,460 --> 01:13:45,950
I just want to highlight we're
going to sort of build up

1628
01:13:45,950 --> 01:13:48,170
in complexity in terms
of the type of problems

1629
01:13:48,170 --> 01:13:49,430
we're talking about.

1630
01:13:49,430 --> 01:13:52,880
So we're going to be thinking
about both like planning

1631
01:13:52,880 --> 01:13:57,500
and control and sort
of thinking about how

1632
01:13:57,500 --> 01:14:01,300
complicated these spaces are.

1633
01:14:01,300 --> 01:14:05,360
So we're going to think
about evaluation and control

1634
01:14:05,360 --> 01:14:08,605
because evaluation is often
a subpart of doing control.

1635
01:14:08,605 --> 01:14:09,980
If you know how
good a policy is,

1636
01:14:09,980 --> 01:14:11,750
you may be able to improve it.

1637
01:14:11,750 --> 01:14:14,570
And then we're going to
talk about tabular function

1638
01:14:14,570 --> 01:14:17,120
approximation
methods, because we're

1639
01:14:17,120 --> 01:14:19,640
going to want to be able to
solve really large problems.

1640
01:14:19,640 --> 01:14:25,130
And then we're going to
talk about both planning

1641
01:14:25,130 --> 01:14:26,570
and learning.

1642
01:14:26,570 --> 01:14:29,270
In planning, we're going
to assume someone gives us

1643
01:14:29,270 --> 01:14:33,050
that dynamics model and that
reward model and the state

1644
01:14:33,050 --> 01:14:33,775
and action space.

1645
01:14:33,775 --> 01:14:36,150
And we're just going to try
to find a really good policy.

1646
01:14:36,150 --> 01:14:38,025
And in learning, we're
going to actually have

1647
01:14:38,025 --> 01:14:39,920
to control the decisions
we make to give us

1648
01:14:39,920 --> 01:14:42,565
information that allows us to
identify an optimal policy.

1649
01:14:45,590 --> 01:14:46,310
All right.

1650
01:14:46,310 --> 01:14:47,870
So what we're
going to start with

1651
01:14:47,870 --> 01:14:49,670
is sort of the simplest of
the settings, which we're

1652
01:14:49,670 --> 01:14:51,795
going to assume that we
have a finite set of states

1653
01:14:51,795 --> 01:14:52,420
and actions.

1654
01:14:52,420 --> 01:14:54,750
We're given models of the
world, meaning someone

1655
01:14:54,750 --> 01:14:57,050
writes down for us
what those look like.

1656
01:14:57,050 --> 01:14:59,930
And we want to evaluate the
performance of the best decision

1657
01:14:59,930 --> 01:15:03,420
policy and then compute
the optimal policy.

1658
01:15:03,420 --> 01:15:07,520
And we can think of this
really as AI planning.

1659
01:15:07,520 --> 01:15:08,380
OK.

1660
01:15:08,380 --> 01:15:09,920
So to think about
how this works,

1661
01:15:09,920 --> 01:15:11,670
we're going to start
with Markov processes

1662
01:15:11,670 --> 01:15:13,460
and then build up to MDPs.

1663
01:15:13,460 --> 01:15:15,290
And this is relevant
because it turns out

1664
01:15:15,290 --> 01:15:18,170
you can think of evaluation as
basically being a Markov reward

1665
01:15:18,170 --> 01:15:20,990
process.

1666
01:15:20,990 --> 01:15:22,820
OK, so how does a
Markov chain work?

1667
01:15:22,820 --> 01:15:24,237
And just raise
your hand if you've

1668
01:15:24,237 --> 01:15:25,630
seen Markov chains before.

1669
01:15:25,630 --> 01:15:26,130
Awesome.

1670
01:15:26,130 --> 01:15:27,797
OK, so most people
have, which is great.

1671
01:15:27,797 --> 01:15:30,110
So this is a memoryless
random process.

1672
01:15:30,110 --> 01:15:31,940
There's no rewards yet.

1673
01:15:31,940 --> 01:15:33,930
There's a finite set
of states in this case.

1674
01:15:33,930 --> 01:15:35,600
And we have a dynamics model.

1675
01:15:35,600 --> 01:15:37,770
And if it's just a
finite set of states,

1676
01:15:37,770 --> 01:15:39,520
we can just write
this down as a matrix.

1677
01:15:41,435 --> 01:15:42,810
Just says, what's
the probability

1678
01:15:42,810 --> 01:15:45,530
of going to the next state
given the previous state?

1679
01:15:45,530 --> 01:15:47,640
And so you could just
have this say in our--

1680
01:15:47,640 --> 01:15:49,560
this would be a Markov
chain transition

1681
01:15:49,560 --> 01:15:54,120
matrix for our Mars rover case.

1682
01:15:54,120 --> 01:15:56,590
And if you wanted to get an
episode, you would just sample.

1683
01:15:56,590 --> 01:15:58,960
So let's say you always
touch down in state S4.

1684
01:15:58,960 --> 01:16:01,260
You just sample episodes
from that particular chain.

1685
01:16:01,260 --> 01:16:02,250
Yeah.

1686
01:16:02,250 --> 01:16:05,010
[INAUDIBLE] rows and
columns down to 1?

1687
01:16:05,010 --> 01:16:08,490
All of the-- and
what's your name?

1688
01:16:08,490 --> 01:16:14,032
Yeah, so all of the
rows have to sum to 1.

1689
01:16:14,032 --> 01:16:14,532
OK.

1690
01:16:17,244 --> 01:16:19,630
Then is it coincidence
that columns sum to 1?

1691
01:16:19,630 --> 01:16:20,130
Yeah.

1692
01:16:20,130 --> 01:16:20,850
OK.

1693
01:16:20,850 --> 01:16:21,557
Yeah.

1694
01:16:21,557 --> 01:16:23,640
I was thinking just now
that I should have changed

1695
01:16:23,640 --> 01:16:26,010
that question because--
and we'll see also

1696
01:16:26,010 --> 01:16:27,605
why that's important later.

1697
01:16:27,605 --> 01:16:29,940
OK, In a Markov
reward process, it's

1698
01:16:29,940 --> 01:16:31,890
a Markov chain plus rewards.

1699
01:16:31,890 --> 01:16:33,960
So same as before.

1700
01:16:33,960 --> 01:16:36,180
But now we have a reward
function that tells us

1701
01:16:36,180 --> 01:16:37,920
how good each of
those states are.

1702
01:16:37,920 --> 01:16:39,970
And we're also going to
have a discount factor.

1703
01:16:39,970 --> 01:16:42,750
And I'll talk about
that in a second.

1704
01:16:42,750 --> 01:16:44,520
We still have no actions.

1705
01:16:44,520 --> 01:16:48,390
And we can express
R as a vector.

1706
01:16:48,390 --> 01:16:51,540
So in this, we could imagine
our Markov reward process

1707
01:16:51,540 --> 01:16:55,630
where we have a plus
1 and S1, 10 in S7.

1708
01:16:55,630 --> 01:16:58,640
So plus 1 [INAUDIBLE] and
0 in all other states.

1709
01:17:01,430 --> 01:17:02,930
In this case, this
is where we start

1710
01:17:02,930 --> 01:17:05,180
to see the ideas that are going
to be really useful for decision

1711
01:17:05,180 --> 01:17:06,888
processes, which is
we can start to think

1712
01:17:06,888 --> 01:17:09,440
about how good particular
trajectories are.

1713
01:17:09,440 --> 01:17:10,815
So we're going to
have a horizon,

1714
01:17:10,815 --> 01:17:12,690
and you're going to see
this in your homework

1715
01:17:12,690 --> 01:17:15,390
2, which is the number of
time steps in each episode.

1716
01:17:15,390 --> 01:17:17,805
It could be infinite,
or it could be finite.

1717
01:17:17,805 --> 01:17:19,430
It's like basically
how many time steps

1718
01:17:19,430 --> 01:17:21,500
do you get to make decisions.

1719
01:17:21,500 --> 01:17:25,040
And the return, which
we're going to call Gt,

1720
01:17:25,040 --> 01:17:27,470
is just going to be the
discounted sum of rewards

1721
01:17:27,470 --> 01:17:29,360
from the time step,
current time step,

1722
01:17:29,360 --> 01:17:31,670
till the end of the horizon.

1723
01:17:31,670 --> 01:17:33,620
And a value function
in this case

1724
01:17:33,620 --> 01:17:36,770
is just going to be
the expected return.

1725
01:17:36,770 --> 01:17:40,400
In general, this is not going to
be the same as the actual return

1726
01:17:40,400 --> 01:17:42,712
unless you just have a
deterministic process,

1727
01:17:42,712 --> 01:17:45,170
because the idea is that you're
going to have stochasticity

1728
01:17:45,170 --> 01:17:46,587
in the trajectories you reach.

1729
01:17:46,587 --> 01:17:49,045
And because of that, you're
going to get different rewards.

1730
01:17:51,920 --> 01:17:52,610
Right.

1731
01:17:52,610 --> 01:17:53,923
So you might wonder--

1732
01:17:53,923 --> 01:17:55,340
if you haven't
seen it before, why

1733
01:17:55,340 --> 01:17:57,120
do we have this
discount factor thing?

1734
01:17:57,120 --> 01:18:00,300
So we're sort of weighing
earlier rewards more

1735
01:18:00,300 --> 01:18:01,280
than later rewards.

1736
01:18:01,280 --> 01:18:03,030
Well, one is that it's
just mathematically

1737
01:18:03,030 --> 01:18:03,948
really convenient.

1738
01:18:03,948 --> 01:18:06,240
It's going to help us not
sum to infinity, particularly

1739
01:18:06,240 --> 01:18:08,590
if we have infinite
number of time steps

1740
01:18:08,590 --> 01:18:10,560
we can make decisions.

1741
01:18:10,560 --> 01:18:12,270
And it turns out
humans often act as

1742
01:18:12,270 --> 01:18:14,200
